const TOPICS = [
  // ─────────────────── SKIP LIST ────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m17", "lld_m17_t3", 3, "Design a Skip List", "design-skip-list",
    ["case-study", "data-structure", "probabilistic", "ordered-map"],
    "You need an ordered map with O(log n) search, insert, and delete, range scans, and — the twist — you must make it thread-safe later without a global lock. A balanced BST gives you the bounds but rebalancing rewrites whole subtrees, which is murder to make concurrent. What if you got the same bounds from a sorted linked list plus a coin?",
    "A skip list is a sorted linked list with express lanes: each node is promoted to level i with probability p^i, decided by coin flips at insert time, so the tower heights form a geometric distribution and a search that descends level by level visits an EXPECTED O(log n) nodes. That word matters — the bound is expected, not worst case; an unlucky run of coin flips genuinely can degrade it. What you buy for accepting a probabilistic bound is that every write is LOCAL: a splice of a handful of forward pointers recorded in an update[] array during the search. No rotations, no subtree rewrites — which is exactly why lock-free ordered maps like ConcurrentSkipListMap are skip lists and not trees.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: an ORDERED MAP over comparable keys supporting get(key), put(key, value) with replace-on-duplicate semantics, remove(key), and — the reason anyone reaches for an ordered structure rather than a hash map — ORDER-DEPENDENT queries: first(), last(), floor(key) and ceiling(key), and a RANGE SCAN from a lower bound that walks forward in sorted order. Iteration must be in ascending key order, and size() should be O(1). Keys need a total order supplied either by Comparable or an injected Comparator; null keys are rejected because the search predicate cannot order them. Non-functional constraints that shape the whole design: search, insert, and delete should be logarithmic in n; memory overhead per element should be a small constant (pointers, not copies); and the structure should be amenable to CONCURRENT access later — many readers, occasional writers — without a single global lock. That last constraint is not decoration: it is the reason this problem exists as a design exercise separate from a balanced BST, which achieves the same asymptotics deterministically. Explicitly out of scope for the first pass: persistence, duplicate keys (a duplicate put replaces the value), and shrink-on-clear policies. State the honest framing up front — a red-black tree is the deterministic answer and TreeMap is the production class; the interview is asking what a randomised structure buys you and what it costs, so the design must own the probabilistic bound rather than pretend it is worst case."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "The object census is deliberately tiny because the difficulty is in the invariant, not the class count. NODE holds an immutable key, a mutable value, and — the one unusual field — a forward ARRAY, forward[0..h], where forward[i] is the next node at level i. A node of height h participates in levels 0 through h and is invisible above h; the array length IS the tower height, fixed at construction, which keeps a node immutable in shape and makes the splice logic uniform. HEAD SENTINEL is a Node with no key and a forward array of MAX_LEVEL entries; it exists so that insertion at the front needs no special case — every search starts at head and every level has an origin. LEVELGENERATOR is the randomiser: randomLevel() flips a biased coin, returning i with probability p^i (1 - p), which is a geometric distribution; it is worth extracting conceptually even if it is three lines, because it is the only source of non-determinism and therefore the only thing a test must be able to seed. The UPDATE ARRAY is not a field but it is a first-class design element: a per-operation scratch array of size MAX_LEVEL where update[i] records the LAST node visited at level i before descending — the predecessor of the insertion point at that level. COMPARATOR carries the total order. SKIPLIST itself owns head, the current maximum occupied level, size, and the generator, and exposes the public map API. Notice what is absent: no parent pointers, no colours, no heights to recompute, no balance factors. That absence is the whole point."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The design act is choosing a PROBABILISTIC INVARIANT over a structural one. A balanced BST maintains a hard invariant (heights differ by at most one; no two consecutive red nodes) and pays for it with rebalancing machinery. A skip list maintains no invariant at all — it maintains a DISTRIBUTION. Each node picks its height once, at birth, from a geometric distribution, and the structure is whatever those flips produced. The claim 'search is O(log n)' is therefore a statement about the expectation over the coin flips, and crucially it is independent of the insertion ORDER: unlike an unbalanced BST, which degenerates to a list on sorted input, a skip list has no adversarial input at all, because the randomness lives in the structure rather than in the data. An adversary who can observe your random source is the only threat, which is why concurrent implementations use a per-thread seeded generator. Structurally the class is a single SkipList owning nodes, with the search routine factored so that get, put, remove, floor and ceiling all reuse the same descent — put and remove additionally recording update[]. This is the closest thing to a pattern here, and it is the right instinct: ONE traversal primitive, three callers, no duplicated pointer walking. Resist introducing a Level class, an ExpressLane abstraction, or a Strategy for the coin — they are objects with no behaviour, and the interviewer reads them as pattern-matching rather than design. Do encapsulate MAX_LEVEL and p as construction parameters with defensible defaults (p = 1/2, MAX_LEVEL = 32 for n up to roughly 2^32), because those two numbers are the tuning surface and being able to say WHY they are what they are is the difference between having read about skip lists and having designed one."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "class SkipList<K extends Comparable<K>, V> {\n  private static final int MAX_LEVEL = 32;\n  private static final double P = 0.5;\n\n  static class Node<K, V> {\n    final K key; V value;\n    final Node<K, V>[] forward;          // forward[i] = next node at level i\n    @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n    Node(K key, V value, int height) {\n      this.key = key; this.value = value;\n      this.forward = new Node[height + 1];   // tower height fixed at birth\n    }\n  }\n\n  private final Node<K, V> head = new Node<>(null, null, MAX_LEVEL);\n  private int level = 0;                 // highest level currently occupied\n  private int size  = 0;\n  private final Random rnd = new Random();\n\n  private int randomLevel() {            // geometric: P(height >= i) = p^i\n    int h = 0;\n    while (h < MAX_LEVEL && rnd.nextDouble() < P) h++;\n    return h;\n  }\n\n  public V get(K key) {\n    Node<K, V> x = head;\n    for (int i = level; i >= 0; i--)                       // descend the tower\n      while (x.forward[i] != null && x.forward[i].key.compareTo(key) < 0)\n        x = x.forward[i];                                  // advance on this level\n    x = x.forward[0];\n    return (x != null && x.key.compareTo(key) == 0) ? x.value : null;\n  }\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  public void put(K key, V value) {\n    Node<K, V>[] update = new Node[MAX_LEVEL + 1];   // predecessor per level\n    Node<K, V> x = head;\n    for (int i = level; i >= 0; i--) {\n      while (x.forward[i] != null && x.forward[i].key.compareTo(key) < 0)\n        x = x.forward[i];\n      update[i] = x;                                 // where we dropped down\n    }\n    Node<K, V> next = x.forward[0];\n    if (next != null && next.key.compareTo(key) == 0) { next.value = value; return; }\n\n    int h = randomLevel();\n    if (h > level) {\n      for (int i = level + 1; i <= h; i++) update[i] = head;   // new lanes start at head\n      level = h;\n    }\n    Node<K, V> n = new Node<>(key, value, h);\n    for (int i = 0; i <= h; i++) {                   // O(1) splice per level\n      n.forward[i] = update[i].forward[i];\n      update[i].forward[i] = n;\n    }\n    size++;\n  }\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  public boolean remove(K key) {\n    Node<K, V>[] update = new Node[MAX_LEVEL + 1];\n    Node<K, V> x = head;\n    for (int i = level; i >= 0; i--) {\n      while (x.forward[i] != null && x.forward[i].key.compareTo(key) < 0)\n        x = x.forward[i];\n      update[i] = x;\n    }\n    Node<K, V> target = x.forward[0];\n    if (target == null || target.key.compareTo(key) != 0) return false;\n    for (int i = 0; i <= level; i++) {\n      if (update[i].forward[i] != target) break;     // target absent above here\n      update[i].forward[i] = target.forward[i];      // unlink, one level at a time\n    }\n    while (level > 0 && head.forward[level] == null) level--;   // shrink the tower\n    size--;\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  public List<Map.Entry<K, V>> range(K from, K to) {\n    List<Map.Entry<K, V>> out = new ArrayList<>();\n    Node<K, V> x = head;\n    for (int i = level; i >= 0; i--)\n      while (x.forward[i] != null && x.forward[i].key.compareTo(from) < 0) x = x.forward[i];\n    for (Node<K, V> n = x.forward[0]; n != null && n.key.compareTo(to) <= 0; n = n.forward[0])\n      out.add(Map.entry(n.key, n.value));            // O(log n) seek + O(k) walk\n    return out;\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the coin flips — why the bound is EXPECTED O(log n), and the argument for it",
        "This is the block the interview is actually about, so derive it rather than assert it. randomLevel() flips a coin with success probability p = 1/2 and counts successes, so a node reaches height at least i with probability p^i. Two consequences follow immediately. First, the EXPECTED NUMBER OF NODES at level i is n * p^i, which means level 0 holds n nodes, level 1 about n/2, level 2 about n/4, and the highest level with any node at all is around log base 1/p of n — roughly log2 n when p = 1/2. Second, the EXPECTED HEIGHT of a single node is 1/(1-p) = 2, so the total pointer count is about 2n: the express lanes cost a constant factor of memory, not an asymptotic one. Now the search cost. The clean argument runs the search BACKWARDS from the target. Stand at the found node and retrace the path: at each step you either climbed a level (because the node you are at is as tall as the current level, probability p) or you moved left one node (probability 1 - p). The expected number of horizontal steps taken before climbing once is 1/p = 2, and you must climb about log2 n times to reach the top, so the expected path length is about 2 log2 n — that is the O(log n), and it falls out of a geometric-distribution expectation, not from any structural guarantee. Say the word EXPECTED and then say what it does not mean: there is no promise for any individual operation. A pathological run of coin flips can make every node height 0, collapsing the structure to a plain sorted linked list with O(n) search. The probability of that is vanishing — the probability that the height exceeds c*log n falls off polynomially in n, so the bound holds with high probability, and a run of a million operations will not visibly miss it — but it is a probability, not a proof, and the distinction is precisely what separates a skip list from an AVL tree in an interview answer. The practical corollary: MAX_LEVEL should be capped (32 covers n up to about 4 billion at p = 1/2) so a freak run cannot allocate an absurd tower, and p is a tuning knob — smaller p means fewer pointers and less memory but longer horizontal runs, so p = 1/2 or 1/4 are the standard picks, with 1/4 preferred when memory matters."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the update[] array — why search must record its path for insert and delete to be O(1) per level",
        "The single most common failure when someone implements a skip list from memory is writing a correct search and then discovering they cannot insert. Here is why. To splice a new node in at level i, you need the node that PRECEDES the insertion point at level i, because that is the node whose forward[i] must be rewritten. The search descends from the top, and at each level it stops advancing exactly at that predecessor before dropping down — so the information is available during the search and unavailable afterwards, because the links are one-directional and there are no parent or backward pointers to climb. If you throw the path away, recovering it means re-searching once per level: O(log n) searches, each O(log n), turning a logarithmic insert into O(log^2 n) and, more damningly, revealing that you did not understand what the descent was doing. The fix is one line inside the descent — update[i] = x after the inner while — and it converts the pointer surgery into pure local work: for each of the new node's h + 1 levels, two assignments, n.forward[i] = update[i].forward[i] followed by update[i].forward[i] = n. That is O(1) per level and the node's height is expected 2, so the SPLICE COST IS EXPECTED CONSTANT; the O(log n) of an insert is entirely the search, not the mutation. Three details separate a working implementation from a nearly-working one. (1) When the new node's random height exceeds the list's current level, the levels above have never been used and update[i] holds null — those entries must be filled with head, and the list's level raised, before splicing, or you dereference null. (2) In remove, the loop must BREAK the first time update[i].forward[i] is not the target: a node of height 1 does not appear at level 5, and blindly rewriting forward[5] would unlink the wrong node and corrupt the level. (3) After a removal, the top levels may be empty; walking level down while head.forward[level] is null keeps the descent from wasting steps on dead lanes. Note the symmetry that makes the structure pleasant: search, insert, delete, floor, ceiling and range all share ONE descent; only the callers differ in what they do at the bottom. That is the reuse an interviewer wants to see factored, and it is why the update array — a humble scratch buffer — is genuinely part of the design rather than an implementation detail."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: why skip lists win on concurrency, plus the follow-ups to expect",
        "Ask why java.util.concurrent ships ConcurrentSkipListMap and not a concurrent red-black tree, and the answer is the shape of a write. In a balanced tree, an insert can trigger rotations that rewrite a subtree: parent, child, grandchild and their links all change, and the change can PROPAGATE toward the root. To make that safe you must either lock a region whose extent you cannot predict before you start, or hold a lock at the root — and a root lock serialises every writer, destroying the point. In a skip list, a write is a bounded number of independent single-pointer updates at known locations, each of which can be done with a compare-and-swap, and each level can be published separately. Lock-free skip lists exploit exactly this: insert links the node at level 0 FIRST — that is the linearisation point, the instant the key becomes visible — then links the higher levels opportunistically, since the upper lanes are only an index and a partially-linked tower is merely slower, never wrong. Deletion mirrors it with a two-phase MARK-THEN-UNLINK: the node's forward pointers are logically marked (in Java, by CASing in a marker node) so no concurrent insert can splice a new node onto a dying one — the classic Harris list-deletion problem — and physical unlinking happens afterwards, possibly by a later traversal. Readers never lock at all; they may traverse a node mid-deletion and simply skip it. That is a concurrency story you can tell in ninety seconds and it is the real reason to choose this structure. FOLLOW-UPS to expect. 'Skip list versus red-black tree?' — same expected bounds, tree gives worst-case guarantees, skip list gives simpler code (no rotations, roughly a third of the lines), better range-scan locality at level 0, and far friendlier concurrency; trees win on memory (no towers) and on hard real-time requirements. 'What is the worst case?' — O(n), with vanishing probability; own it. 'Can an adversary force it?' — only by predicting your random source, so seed per instance or per thread and never derive the height from the key. 'Why not just a sorted array?' — O(log n) binary search but O(n) insert; the skip list trades search constant factors for cheap mutation. 'How would you make range queries efficient?' — they already are: one O(log n) descent to the lower bound, then a linear walk at level 0, which is why databases and LSM memtables use skip lists. 'Add rank queries (the k-th element)?' — store a span count on each forward pointer, the number of level-0 nodes it skips; maintaining spans during splices gives O(log n) select and rank, and that extension is a favourite follow-up."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "PROBABILISTIC vs DETERMINISTIC BALANCE: the honest framing is that a skip list buys implementation simplicity and concurrency-friendliness by giving up a worst-case guarantee. For a latency SLO measured at the 99.9th percentile over millions of operations the difference is invisible; for a hard real-time system, or for a security-sensitive context where an adversary might influence timing, the deterministic red-black tree is the correct choice. MEMORY: expected 2n forward pointers at p = 1/2 versus 2n child pointers plus a colour bit per node in a red-black tree — comparable, though the skip list allocates a variable-length array per node, which fragments more and hurts cache locality on the upper lanes. Dropping p to 1/4 cuts expected pointers per node to 1.33 at the cost of about 33 percent longer searches, and that dial is worth mentioning as a real tuning decision. CACHE BEHAVIOUR: the level-0 walk is beautifully sequential, which is why range scans are strong, but the descent chases pointers across the heap; a B-tree or B+ tree, packing many keys per cache-line-friendly node, beats both skip lists and BSTs for on-disk and large in-memory ordered indexes, and saying so shows you know where this structure stops being the answer. THREAD SAFETY LADDER: a single lock (trivial, serialises everything), a read-write lock (fine when reads dominate and writes are rare), per-level or per-node fine-grained locking (Herlihy's lazy skip list), or fully lock-free with mark-then-unlink deletion — pick by contention, and note that only the last two exploit what the structure is actually good at. EXTENSIONS worth naming: SPAN COUNTS per forward pointer give O(log n) rank and select, which is how Redis sorted sets are built; DUPLICATE KEYS need a secondary tie-break in the comparator (Redis orders by score then member) rather than duplicate nodes; a DETERMINISTIC skip list (1-2-3 skip list) restores worst-case bounds by enforcing a rule on consecutive same-height gaps, at the cost of reintroducing rebalancing — which is a neat way to say that determinism and locality are the two ends of this particular see-saw."),
    ],
    "Skip lists are the standard interview probe for whether a candidate can reason about EXPECTED versus WORST-CASE complexity, and most answers fail on exactly that word: they recite O(log n) without noticing that a run of coin flips could produce a linked list. The strong answer derives the bound from the geometric distribution of tower heights, explains that randomness in the STRUCTURE (not the data) is why insertion order cannot degrade it, shows the update[] array as the thing that makes insert and delete O(1) pointer surgery per level, and closes with the reason the structure exists in production at all — writes are local, so lock-free and fine-grained-locking implementations are tractable in a way a rotating tree never is. Interviewers commonly follow with rank queries via span counts, or with 'why did ConcurrentSkipListMap not use a tree', and both reward the same understanding.",
    [
      G("expected_vs_worst_case", "Stating skip list operations as O(log n) worst case, the same as a balanced tree, with no mention of the probability distribution.", "Say EXPECTED O(log n) and justify it: node height is geometric with P(height >= i) = p^i, so about log base 1/p of n levels exist and the backward search argument gives an expected 1/p horizontal steps per level; the worst case is genuinely O(n) with vanishing probability.", "complexity table listing skip list and AVL identically; no mention of randomLevel or the coin; claiming sorted input degrades a skip list (it does not — the randomness is in the structure, not the data)."),
      G("no_update_array", "Writing the descent for search but re-searching or scanning to find predecessors when inserting or deleting, or attempting to climb back up with parent pointers that do not exist.", "Record update[i] = last node visited at level i during the single descent; insert and delete then rewrite exactly two pointers per level, making the mutation expected O(1) per level and the whole operation O(log n) from the search alone.", "insert calling search once per level; O(log^2 n) insert; a Node class with a back or parent pointer; delete rewriting forward[i] without checking update[i].forward[i] == target first."),
      G("misses_concurrency_rationale", "Presenting the skip list as merely a simpler alternative to a balanced tree, with no account of why production concurrent ordered maps are skip lists.", "Name the mechanism: a write is a bounded set of independent single-pointer CAS updates at known locations, level 0 linked first as the linearisation point and deletion done as mark-then-unlink — a rotating tree instead rewrites an unpredictable region, which is why fine-grained or lock-free versions of it are impractical.", "cannot explain ConcurrentSkipListMap; proposes a single global lock as the only thread-safety story; claims a concurrent red-black tree would be equally easy."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.skiplist, "Skip list class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── CIRCULAR BUFFER (RING BUFFER) ────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m17", "lld_m17_t4", 4, "Design a Circular Buffer (Ring Buffer)", "design-circular-buffer",
    ["case-study", "data-structure", "concurrency", "systems"],
    "A telemetry agent must keep the last 4,096 log lines in memory with zero allocation on the hot path, one thread writing and one thread draining. You reach for an array and two indices — and immediately hit the question that has sunk a thousand implementations: when head equals tail, is the buffer empty or completely full? Design the ring buffer, and answer that.",
    "head == tail is genuinely ambiguous, and every correct ring buffer resolves it by adding information: a SIZE COUNTER, a SACRIFICED SLOT (never fill the last one, so full means head == (tail + 1) mod cap), or MONOTONIC SEQUENCE NUMBERS that never wrap, where size = tail - head. Pick one deliberately and the rest of the design falls out. Two more decisions carry real weight: a power-of-two capacity turns the wrap modulo into a single bitmask AND, and the overwrite-oldest versus reject-when-full behaviour is a CONTRACT, not an implementation detail — it is the difference between a lossy telemetry ring and a lossless work queue.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a FIXED-CAPACITY FIFO over a pre-allocated array. offer(item) appends at the tail; poll() removes and returns the head; peek() reads the head without removing; size(), isEmpty(), isFull() report state; clear() resets. The behaviour WHEN FULL must be specified by the caller, not assumed: either REJECT (offer returns false, the producer applies backpressure) or OVERWRITE-OLDEST (the write always succeeds and the oldest unread element is silently dropped, advancing the head). Both are legitimate and they serve different systems — a work queue must not lose tasks; a crash-dump ring or an audio jitter buffer must never block the producer and would rather lose the oldest sample. Some rings also want indexed access, get(i) meaning the i-th oldest element, which a linked queue cannot offer at all. Non-functional constraints are what make this a design question rather than an exercise: ZERO ALLOCATION after construction, since the entire point is to avoid per-item garbage on a hot path; O(1) worst case for every operation, no amortisation and no copying; predictable MEMORY, exactly capacity slots plus a few fields; and, in most real uses, safe hand-off between exactly one producer thread and one consumer thread WITHOUT a lock — a single-producer single-consumer ring is the classic lock-free structure, and the interviewer will steer there. Clarify explicitly: is capacity fixed forever (yes, by definition — a resizable ring is really a growable deque and a different design), and is the element type primitive or reference (references need null-out on removal to avoid retaining garbage)."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "RINGBUFFER<T> owns a final T[] slots array allocated once at construction, and two position fields. The naming choice matters and is worth stating: HEAD (also called read index) is where the next poll() reads; TAIL (write index) is where the next offer() writes. Whether these are WRAPPED indices in [0, capacity) or MONOTONIC SEQUENCES that only ever increase is the first fork in the design, and it determines everything downstream — with monotonic sequences the array index is derived on use, seq & mask, and size is simply tail - head with no ambiguity to resolve. CAPACITY is final, and if the power-of-two optimisation is taken there is a derived MASK field equal to capacity - 1, plus a constructor that rounds the requested capacity up to the next power of two and says so in its contract. OVERFLOWPOLICY captures the full-buffer contract; for two behaviours an enum plus a branch is the right amount of machinery, and reaching for a Strategy interface with two implementations is over-engineering worth naming as such in the interview. For the concurrent variant, the entities barely change but their MEMORY SEMANTICS do: head and tail become volatile (or VarHandle acquire/release accessors), and each is written by exactly ONE thread — the producer owns tail, the consumer owns head — which is the invariant that makes the lock-free version correct. Optional PADDING fields around head and tail exist purely to place them on different cache lines. What is deliberately absent: no per-item node objects, no size field if sequences are used, no lock in the SPSC configuration."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "There is no design pattern here in the Gang-of-Four sense, and saying that plainly is better than manufacturing one; what there is instead is a set of three decisions that must be made explicitly. DECISION ONE, resolving the head == tail ambiguity — the mechanism the whole structure hinges on, treated in full in the deep dive below. DECISION TWO, index arithmetic: (i + 1) % capacity is correct for any capacity but a hardware division, tens of cycles and not pipelined; constraining capacity to a POWER OF TWO lets you write (i + 1) & mask instead, one cycle, and the reason it works is that for capacity = 2^k the low k bits of any integer are exactly its value modulo capacity. The cost is that a caller asking for 1,000 slots gets 1,024 — a documented rounding, not a lie. Note the trap: masking is only valid for non-negative values, so if you use monotonic sequences you must use long counters (a 64-bit sequence at a billion operations per second takes almost 300 years to overflow) rather than int, or handle wraparound explicitly. DECISION THREE, the full policy, which belongs in the constructor as a declared contract because it changes what callers must handle: under REJECT, offer returns false and every caller must check it; under OVERWRITE, offer returns void and the consumer must tolerate gaps — a ring used for metrics should even expose a droppedCount so the loss is observable rather than silent. The public API should keep the two shapes distinguishable rather than papering over them: offer() returning boolean for the lossless ring, and a separate write() or add() that always succeeds for the lossy one. Encapsulation rule: head, tail and the array are private and no method hands out the internal array, because a leaked reference lets a caller observe or corrupt slots the buffer considers free."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "/** Capacity rounded up to a power of two; size = tail - head (monotonic seqs). */\nclass RingBuffer<T> {\n  private final T[] slots;\n  private final int mask;                // capacity - 1, capacity = 2^k\n  private long head = 0;                 // next index to READ  (consumer owns)\n  private long tail = 0;                 // next index to WRITE (producer owns)\n  private final boolean overwrite;       // full policy: contract, not detail\n  private long dropped = 0;\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  RingBuffer(int requested, boolean overwriteOldest) {\n    int cap = Integer.highestOneBit(Math.max(1, requested - 1)) << 1;  // next 2^k\n    this.slots = (T[]) new Object[cap];\n    this.mask = cap - 1;\n    this.overwrite = overwriteOldest;\n  }\n\n  public int size()      { return (int) (tail - head); }   // never ambiguous\n  public boolean isEmpty(){ return tail == head; }\n  public boolean isFull() { return tail - head == slots.length; }\n\n  public boolean offer(T item) {\n    if (isFull()) {\n      if (!overwrite) return false;                 // REJECT: backpressure\n      slots[(int) (head & mask)] = null;            // OVERWRITE: drop oldest\n      head++;\n      dropped++;\n    }\n    slots[(int) (tail & mask)] = item;              // & mask, not % capacity\n    tail++;\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  public T poll() {\n    if (isEmpty()) return null;\n    int i = (int) (head & mask);\n    T item = slots[i];\n    slots[i] = null;                                // release the reference\n    head++;\n    return item;\n  }\n\n  public T get(int i) {                             // i-th oldest, O(1)\n    if (i < 0 || i >= size()) throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException();\n    return slots[(int) ((head + i) & mask)];\n  }\n}\n\n/** Single-producer / single-consumer, lock-free: no mutual exclusion needed,\n *  only VISIBILITY. Producer writes tail only; consumer writes head only. */\nclass SpscRingBuffer<T> {\n  private final T[] slots;\n  private final int mask;\n  private volatile long head = 0;        // written by consumer, read by producer\n  private volatile long tail = 0;        // written by producer, read by consumer\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  SpscRingBuffer(int capacityPow2) {\n    slots = (T[]) new Object[capacityPow2];\n    mask = capacityPow2 - 1;\n  }\n\n  public boolean offer(T item) {                    // PRODUCER thread only\n    long t = tail;                                  // plain read: we own it\n    if (t - head == slots.length) return false;     // volatile read of head\n    slots[(int) (t & mask)] = item;                 // publish the slot FIRST...\n    tail = t + 1;                                   // ...then the release store\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  public T poll() {                                 // CONSUMER thread only\n    long h = head;\n    if (h == tail) return null;                     // volatile read of tail\n    int i = (int) (h & mask);\n    T item = slots[i];\n    slots[i] = null;\n    head = h + 1;                                   // release store\n    return item;\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: head == tail — the ambiguity and its three standard resolutions",
        "Two indices into an array of capacity C can express C distinct differences (0 through C-1) but the buffer has C+1 distinct occupancy states (0 through C elements). The pigeonhole conflict is unavoidable: head == tail must mean two different things, empty and completely full, and no amount of clever comparison recovers the missing bit. Every correct implementation therefore ADDS INFORMATION, and there are exactly three standard ways. RESOLUTION ONE, THE SIZE COUNTER: keep an explicit int count incremented on write and decremented on read; empty is count == 0, full is count == capacity, and all C+1 slots are usable. It is the clearest to read and the easiest to get right, and it is what ArrayBlockingQueue does. Its cost is concurrency: count is written by BOTH the producer and the consumer, so in a multithreaded ring it is a shared mutable field requiring atomics or a lock — which is precisely the contention the lock-free design is trying to avoid, and this is why the simplest single-threaded answer is the wrong answer for SPSC. RESOLUTION TWO, THE SACRIFICED SLOT: never allow the buffer to hold more than capacity - 1 items, so head == tail unambiguously means empty and full becomes (tail + 1) & mask == head. No extra field, no shared counter, and each index is still written by only one thread — excellent for concurrency. The costs are one wasted slot (irrelevant at 4,096, annoying at 4) and arithmetic that reads slightly less obviously. RESOLUTION THREE, MONOTONIC SEQUENCE NUMBERS: let head and tail be 64-bit counters that NEVER wrap, deriving the array index as seq & mask only at the point of access. Now size is literally tail - head, empty is tail == head, full is tail - head == capacity — all C+1 states expressible, no shared counter, each sequence owned by one thread, and as a bonus the sequence number is a globally unique, monotonically increasing id for every item ever written, which is exactly what a Disruptor uses to coordinate multiple consumers. This is the resolution the code above takes and the one to reach for by default; its only caveats are that the counters must be long (int would wrap in seconds at high throughput) and that the masking arithmetic assumes non-negative values. The interviewer's probe is usually 'so is it empty or full?' — the strong answer names all three resolutions, picks one, and justifies the pick by the threading model rather than by taste."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the overwrite-vs-reject contract, and power-of-two masking",
        "THE FULL POLICY IS A CONTRACT, and the most common design failure in this problem is treating it as an implementation detail chosen by whoever wrote the method. Consider the two systems. A TASK QUEUE feeding a thread pool must never overwrite: a dropped task is a lost payment, a lost email, a lost job. Full means the producer must be told — offer returns false — and the correct system behaviour is BACKPRESSURE, either blocking the producer or shedding load explicitly upstream where the decision is visible. A TELEMETRY RING, a flight-recorder log, or an audio jitter buffer wants the opposite: the producer is a real-time thread that must never block, stale data is worthless anyway, and the newest sample matters more than the oldest, so the write always succeeds and the head advances over the unread element. The two policies produce different APIs (boolean offer versus void write), different caller obligations (check the return versus tolerate gaps), and different observability requirements — a lossy ring should expose a dropped counter and, ideally, a sequence number gap the consumer can detect, because silent loss is the failure mode that gets discovered during an outage postmortem. Never let the policy be implicit; put it in the constructor and in the Javadoc. The subtle correctness point for OVERWRITE is that the head must be advanced BEFORE or atomically with the write of the new element, and in a concurrent setting overwrite-oldest is genuinely hard because the consumer may be reading the very slot the producer is about to reuse — which is why lossy concurrent rings either accept torn reads for primitives or use sequence-number validation (read seq, read data, re-read seq, retry if it changed — a seqlock). POWER-OF-TWO MASKING is the second mechanism. Integer division and modulo are among the slowest common ALU operations, and the wrap happens on every single access. Constraining capacity to 2^k makes index = seq & (capacity - 1), which is exact because the low k bits of a value ARE that value modulo 2^k. The constructor rounds up rather than rejecting odd sizes, and documents it. Two footguns to name: the trick is invalid for negative operands, so signed counters must be kept non-negative or the mask must be applied to a long before the int cast; and if you ever allow a non-power-of-two capacity as a fallback, the two paths must not silently diverge — keep one arithmetic helper, not two."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the SPSC lock-free case, false sharing, and the follow-ups",
        "The single-producer single-consumer ring is the crown jewel of this design because it needs NO MUTUAL EXCLUSION AT ALL — and understanding why is the senior signal. The producer is the only writer of tail; the consumer is the only writer of head. There is no shared mutable variable with two writers, so there is no lost update to prevent and therefore no lock to take. What remains is purely a VISIBILITY and ORDERING problem: the consumer must not observe the incremented tail before it can observe the data written into the slot, or it will read a stale or null element. In Java the fix is that head and tail are volatile: writing tail is a RELEASE store, which guarantees that everything written before it (the slot assignment) is visible to any thread that performs the matching ACQUIRE load of tail. That is why the code writes slots[...] = item FIRST and tail = t + 1 second — reverse those two lines and the structure is broken in a way that will pass every single-threaded test and fail on a weakly-ordered CPU under load. The equivalent in C or C++ is store-release and load-acquire on the indices; on x86 the hardware already gives you most of this and the bug hides even better. FALSE SHARING is the performance half. If head and tail land on the same 64-byte cache line, every producer increment of tail invalidates the consumer's cached copy of the line that also holds head, and vice versa — the two threads ping-pong the line between cores thousands of times a second while logically sharing nothing. The fix is PADDING: place head and tail on separate cache lines (in Java, @Contended or manual long padding fields; in C, alignas(64)). A further refinement each side caches the other's index locally and only re-reads the volatile when its cached view says the buffer is full or empty, cutting cross-core traffic dramatically — that is the core Disruptor trick. FOLLOW-UPS to expect: 'Why not just use ArrayBlockingQueue?' — you should, unless you need overwrite semantics, zero allocation with no lock, or SPSC throughput; ABQ takes a lock and maintains a shared count. 'Make it multi-producer' — now tail IS contended and you need a CAS to claim a slot (claim the sequence, write, then publish it), which is where the Disruptor's availability array or a two-phase publish comes in. 'How do you resize it?' — you do not; a ring is fixed by definition, and needing growth means you wanted a growable deque, whose amortised doubling is a different design. 'What about the null-out on poll?' — for reference types, failing to null the slot retains the object and leaks memory for the buffer's lifetime; for primitives it is a non-issue."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "SIZE COUNTER vs SACRIFICED SLOT vs SEQUENCES: the counter is the most readable and the right choice for a single-threaded or already-locked ring; the sacrificed slot is the cheapest concurrent answer when you cannot afford 64-bit counters; monotonic sequences cost nothing in a modern runtime, expose all capacity, and hand you a free unique id per element, so they are the default for anything concurrent. The trade is entirely about who writes which field. RING vs LINKED QUEUE: the ring pre-allocates and never allocates again, keeps elements contiguous for cache locality and prefetching, and supports O(1) indexed access to the i-th oldest — but its capacity is fixed forever and it wastes memory when usually near-empty. A linked queue grows without bound, which sounds like an advantage until it is the reason a service OOMs instead of applying backpressure; BOUNDEDNESS IS A FEATURE. RING vs ArrayDeque: ArrayDeque is a ring that doubles when full, so it is the growable cousin; use it when you want a deque and can tolerate amortised resize pauses and allocation. LOCK vs LOCK-FREE: a single lock is correct for any producer/consumer count and costs a few tens of nanoseconds uncontended, which is fine for most services; lock-free SPSC is worth the subtlety only on genuinely hot paths (market data, audio, logging agents) where you can also afford the discipline of never letting a second producer touch it — and an SPSC ring used from two producers fails silently, so the constraint must be enforced by construction or by documentation loud enough to survive. EXTENSIONS: batch offer and drain amortise the volatile writes across many elements and are often a bigger win than any micro-optimisation; a BLOCKING variant wraps the ring in a lock and two conditions (which is precisely ArrayBlockingQueue); MULTIPLE INDEPENDENT CONSUMERS each keep their own head sequence and the producer respects the minimum, which is the Disruptor's multicast model; and a SEQLOCK-style read (read sequence, read data, re-read sequence) lets a lossy ring serve readers that must never block, at the price of retry loops."),
    ],
    "The ring buffer is a favourite because it looks like a five-minute warm-up and immediately exposes depth. The head == tail ambiguity is the first filter — a candidate who cannot name at least two of the three resolutions has not implemented one — and the second is whether the full-buffer behaviour is presented as a deliberate CONTRACT (reject for backpressure, overwrite for lossy telemetry) or fumbled as an afterthought. Beyond that, interviewers push into systems territory: power-of-two capacity and masking instead of modulo, and the SPSC lock-free case where the insight is that no mutual exclusion is needed at all because each index has exactly one writer, leaving only memory visibility to solve with volatile or acquire/release, plus cache-line padding to avoid false sharing. It is one of the few LLD prompts where hardware reasoning is not a bonus but the expected answer.",
    [
      G("head_tail_ambiguity", "Using head == tail alone to test emptiness while also letting the buffer fill completely, so a full buffer reads as empty (or a full buffer silently overwrites unread data).", "Add information deliberately: an explicit size counter, a sacrificed slot so full is (tail + 1) & mask == head, or monotonic long sequences where size = tail - head; choose based on which threads write which field.", "isEmpty() and isFull() both reducing to head == tail; a poll that returns null on a full buffer; capacity - 1 usable slots with no explanation of why."),
      G("full_policy_implicit", "Deciding overwrite-oldest or reject-when-full inside the offer method without exposing it, so callers cannot tell whether data loss is possible.", "Make the policy an explicit constructor-level contract with an API that reflects it — boolean offer for the lossless ring, void write plus a dropped counter for the lossy one — and document the caller's obligation either to handle false or to tolerate gaps.", "offer() silently advancing head when full; no dropped/overwritten metric; the same class used for both a task queue and a telemetry ring with no configuration."),
      G("spsc_visibility_ignored", "Claiming an SPSC ring is thread-safe because each index has one writer, without making the indices volatile or ordering the slot write before the index publish.", "State the two halves: no mutual exclusion is needed (one writer per index) but VISIBILITY is — publish the element into the slot first, then perform the release store of the index (volatile write / store-release), and pad head and tail onto separate cache lines to avoid false sharing.", "plain non-volatile long indices in a concurrent ring; tail incremented before the slot is written; head and tail declared adjacently with no padding while claiming high throughput."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.ringbuffer, "Circular buffer class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── BLOOM FILTER ─────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m17", "lld_m17_t5", 5, "Design a Bloom Filter", "design-bloom-filter",
    ["case-study", "data-structure", "probabilistic", "hashing"],
    "A storage engine answers 200,000 key lookups a second, and 95 percent of them are for keys that do not exist — each one costing a disk seek to discover nothing. You have 100 million keys and 200 MB of RAM, nowhere near enough for a hash set. Can you build something that tells you 'definitely not here' in a few nanoseconds, and how wrong is it allowed to be?",
    "A Bloom filter is a bit array of m bits plus k independent hash functions: add(x) sets the k bits that x hashes to, and mightContain(x) returns true only if ALL k are set. The error is ONE-SIDED and that asymmetry is the entire product — a false positive is possible (other elements happened to set those bits) but a false negative is IMPOSSIBLE, because a bit once set is never cleared. That single guarantee is what makes it safe as a pre-filter in front of an expensive lookup: 'no' is final, 'maybe' costs you the lookup you would have done anyway. And because the error rate is a closed-form function of m, k and n, you do not guess the size — you SOLVE for it.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a SET MEMBERSHIP structure over an expected number of elements n, supporting add(element) and mightContain(element). The contract must be stated precisely because it is unusual: mightContain returning FALSE means the element was definitely never added; mightContain returning TRUE means the element was PROBABLY added, with a false-positive probability the caller configures. There is no remove operation in the basic structure and no way to enumerate the contents — the filter stores no elements, only bits, so you can never get anything back out of it. Useful companions: an approximate count of inserted elements, a union of two filters built with identical parameters (a bitwise OR, which is exactly the set union), and serialisation to bytes for shipping the filter over the network — a common real use, since a small filter can travel where the full key set cannot. Non-functional requirements are the design: SPACE is the reason the structure exists, so the filter must be sized from a TARGET FALSE-POSITIVE RATE rather than by feel, and the caller must be told what happens when the real n exceeds the assumed n (the error rate degrades, gracefully but predictably, and eventually the filter saturates to all-ones and answers true for everything); SPEED must be O(k) with k a small constant, independent of n, with no allocation per query; and the structure must be safe to use as a GUARD in front of an expensive operation, which is a requirement about the error being one-sided and never the other way round. Clarify at the start whether deletions are needed — if yes, the design changes to a counting variant and the space analysis changes with it."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "BLOOMFILTER owns four things and very little behaviour, which is a hint that the intelligence is in the sizing rather than in the object graph. The BIT ARRAY is m bits, in Java best held as a long[] of length ceil(m/64) with manual indexing (word = i >>> 6, bit = 1L << i) rather than a BitSet, because you want a fixed allocation and predictable memory; the array starts all zeros and bits are only ever set, never cleared, which is the invariant that guarantees no false negatives. The parameters M (bits) and K (number of hash functions) are final and are DERIVED, not chosen: the constructor takes the expected element count n and the target false-positive probability p, and computes m and k from them. The HASH FUNCTIONS are the third piece; the interface you actually need is 'give me k independent-looking indices in [0, m) for this element', and the standard implementation derives them from just TWO base hashes by double hashing rather than running k separate algorithms. A HASHER abstraction earns its place here — the filter should not care whether elements are strings, byte arrays or domain objects, so an injected Funnel or toBytes strategy that turns T into bytes is the one genuinely useful indirection. An INSERTED COUNTER is worth keeping for observability, so the filter can report its estimated current error rate and warn when it is being used past its design capacity. COUNTINGBLOOMFILTER is the sibling entity to name: the same layout with 4-bit counters instead of single bits, which trades four times the space for a remove operation. Notice the negative space: no element storage, no buckets, no chains — the filter is intentionally amnesiac, and that is what buys the compression."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The design act is SIZING, and the strongest signal a candidate can give is refusing to pick m and k by intuition. The relationship is closed-form. After inserting n elements with k hash functions into m bits, the probability that a specific bit is still zero is (1 - 1/m)^(kn), which is approximately e^(-kn/m), so the probability that all k bits of a non-member are set — the FALSE POSITIVE RATE — is p ~ (1 - e^(-kn/m))^k. Two useful inversions follow. Given n and a target p, the required bits are m = -(n * ln p) / (ln 2)^2, and the optimal number of hash functions is k = (m/n) * ln 2. Put numbers on it in the interview, because that is what makes it real: p = 1 percent needs about 9.6 bits per element with k = 7; p = 0.1 percent needs about 14.4 bits with k = 10. Nine and a half BITS per element against a hash set's dozens of BYTES per element is the whole value proposition, and it does not depend on how long the keys are — a 200-byte URL and a 4-byte integer cost the same. The intuition behind optimal k is worth articulating: too few hash functions and a non-member clears the test too easily; too many and each insert sets so many bits that the array saturates, driving the false-positive rate back up — the optimum is where about half the bits are set. Structurally the class stays a single final class with a static factory (BloomFilter.create(n, p)) rather than a constructor taking m and k directly, because the factory is where the mathematics lives and it prevents callers from inventing parameters. Immutability of parameters is enforced; the only mutable state is the bit array and the counter. Do not build a Strategy hierarchy for hash functions — one injected byte-conversion function plus double hashing is the entire requirement, and extra interfaces here read as pattern-application rather than design."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "class BloomFilter<T> {\n  private final long[] words;            // m bits packed into 64-bit words\n  private final int m;                   // bit count\n  private final int k;                   // number of hash functions\n  private final Function<T, byte[]> toBytes;\n  private int inserted = 0;\n\n  private BloomFilter(int m, int k, Function<T, byte[]> toBytes) {\n    this.m = m; this.k = k; this.toBytes = toBytes;\n    this.words = new long[(m + 63) >>> 6];\n  }\n\n  /** Size the filter from expected element count n and target false-positive rate p. */\n  static <T> BloomFilter<T> create(int n, double p, Function<T, byte[]> toBytes) {\n    if (n <= 0 || p <= 0 || p >= 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n    int m = (int) Math.ceil(-n * Math.log(p) / (Math.log(2) * Math.log(2)));\n    int k = Math.max(1, (int) Math.round((double) m / n * Math.log(2)));\n    return new BloomFilter<>(m, k, toBytes);\n  }\n\n  /** Kirsch-Mitzenmacher double hashing: k indices from TWO base hashes. */\n  private int indexAt(int i, long h1, long h2) {\n    long combined = h1 + (long) i * h2;\n    return (int) Math.floorMod(combined, (long) m);   // never negative\n  }\n\n  public void add(T element) {\n    byte[] b = toBytes.apply(element);\n    long h1 = Hashing.murmur3(b, 0), h2 = Hashing.murmur3(b, 0x9E3779B9);\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n      int bit = indexAt(i, h1, h2);\n      words[bit >>> 6] |= 1L << bit;     // set only — bits are NEVER cleared\n    }\n    inserted++;\n  }\n\n  public boolean mightContain(T element) {\n    byte[] b = toBytes.apply(element);\n    long h1 = Hashing.murmur3(b, 0), h2 = Hashing.murmur3(b, 0x9E3779B9);\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n      int bit = indexAt(i, h1, h2);\n      if ((words[bit >>> 6] & (1L << bit)) == 0)\n        return false;                    // ONE zero bit = definitely absent\n    }\n    return true;                         // all set = probably present\n  }\n\n  /** Current expected false-positive rate given how many elements are in. */\n  public double currentFpp() {\n    return Math.pow(1 - Math.exp(-(double) k * inserted / m), k);\n  }\n\n  /** Union of two filters built with IDENTICAL m and k is a bitwise OR. */\n  public void union(BloomFilter<T> other) {\n    if (other.m != m || other.k != k) throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n    for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) words[i] |= other.words[i];\n  }\n}\n\n/** Deletion needs counters, not bits: 4 bits per slot, decrement on remove. */\nclass CountingBloomFilter<T> {\n  private final byte[] counters;         // 4-bit counters packed two per byte\n  // add(x):    for each of k slots, increment (saturating at 15)\n  // remove(x): for each of k slots, decrement if > 0\n  // contains:  all k counters > 0\n  // Cost: 4x the memory; risk: counter overflow makes removal unsound.\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: sizing the filter — the false-positive mathematics, used for real",
        "The reason this structure appears in senior interviews is that it is one of the few data structures whose ERROR IS AN ENGINEERING PARAMETER, and the expected competence is being able to turn a business requirement into m and k. Derive it rather than quoting it. Insert n elements, each setting k bits, so kn bit-set operations occur. The probability a given bit is NOT set by one operation is (1 - 1/m); after kn operations it is (1 - 1/m)^(kn), which for large m is approximately e^(-kn/m). So the probability that a given bit IS set is 1 - e^(-kn/m). A query for a non-member checks k bits, and it returns a false positive only if all k happen to be set, giving p ~ (1 - e^(-kn/m))^k. (The standard caveat, worth a sentence if the interviewer is mathematical: this treats the k bit tests as independent, which they are not exactly — the true value is very slightly higher — but the approximation is excellent for realistic m.) Now invert. Minimising p over k for fixed m and n gives k* = (m/n) ln 2 ~ 0.693 * (m/n), and substituting back gives the memory requirement m = -(n ln p) / (ln 2)^2 ~ -1.44 * n * log2(p). Translate that into numbers you can say out loud: 9.6 bits per element for 1 percent, 14.4 for 0.1 percent, 19.2 for 0.01 percent — each additional factor of ten in accuracy costs about 4.8 more bits per element, which is a beautifully cheap curve and exactly why the structure is everywhere. For the 100-million-key scenario at 1 percent that is 960 million bits, about 120 MB, comfortably inside 200 MB of RAM where a hash set of the keys would need many gigabytes. The interpretation of optimal k is the part people miss: at k*, approximately HALF the bits in the array are set, which is the information-theoretic sweet spot — fewer hash functions leave the array sparse and make membership too easy to fake, more hash functions saturate it and every query starts finding all its bits set. Three practical consequences follow. First, m and k are fixed at construction from an ASSUMED n; if the real n comes in double, p does not double, it degrades according to the formula (roughly squaring the per-bit occupancy effect) — so expose currentFpp() and alarm on it. Second, you cannot resize a Bloom filter in place, because the bit positions are functions of m; growth means building a new filter and re-adding every element, which requires the source data, or using a SCALABLE Bloom filter, a chain of filters with geometrically decreasing target rates. Third, the false-positive rate is independent of KEY SIZE, which is why the structure crushes a hash set for long keys like URLs."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the one-sided error contract, and why deletion is impossible",
        "THE ONE-SIDED GUARANTEE is the reason the structure is safe to deploy, and it deserves to be stated as an invariant rather than as a property: a bit, once set, is never cleared, therefore every bit that an added element set is still set, therefore mightContain can never return false for an element that was added. NO FALSE NEGATIVES, EVER. That is what licenses the entire usage pattern — the filter goes IN FRONT of an expensive authoritative lookup, and only the negative answer is trusted. In the storage-engine scenario, a false says 'skip the disk seek' and is always correct; a true says 'go look', and if it was a false positive you pay exactly the seek you would have paid anyway. The system is therefore never WRONG, only occasionally not-optimised, which is a completely different risk profile from a cache that can serve stale data. This is why LSM-tree engines (LevelDB, RocksDB, Cassandra, HBase) put a Bloom filter on every SSTable, why Chrome once shipped one for malicious-URL checks, why CDNs use one to answer 'is this object worth a origin fetch', and why Medium uses one for 'has this reader already seen this post'. Say the deployment shape out loud, because the mathematics without the usage pattern reads as trivia. NOW THE DELETION PROBLEM. The obvious idea — to remove x, clear its k bits — is unsound, and the reason is bit SHARING. Suppose x and y both hash to bit 42 (among others). Clearing bit 42 while removing x silently removes y as well, and now mightContain(y) returns false for an element that IS in the set: a FALSE NEGATIVE, which destroys the one guarantee the structure exists to provide and, in a storage engine, means a key that genuinely exists is reported absent and the read returns nothing. Because the filter stores no elements, it cannot check whether any other element needs bit 42, and there is no cheap fix within the bit-array representation. The standard resolutions are three. (1) COUNTING BLOOM FILTER: replace each bit with a small counter, typically 4 bits; add increments, remove decrements, membership tests counters greater than zero. It works, costs four times the memory, and introduces its own failure — counter overflow, since a 4-bit counter saturates at 15 and a saturated counter can no longer be safely decremented, so removals after saturation reintroduce false negatives. (2) REBUILD: keep the authoritative data elsewhere and periodically regenerate the filter from scratch, which is exactly what compaction does in an LSM tree, so deletions are handled by the system, not by the filter. (3) Use a different structure — a CUCKOO FILTER supports deletion, is often smaller than a Bloom filter for false-positive rates below about 3 percent, and stores fingerprints rather than bits. Name all three and pick by whether the caller genuinely needs deletes or merely thinks it does."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: k hashes from two, and the follow-ups to expect",
        "Computing k independent hash functions sounds like it requires k algorithms, and the naive implementations do exactly that — MD5, SHA-1, CRC32, and then people start inventing. That is slow (cryptographic hashes are enormously more expensive than needed), hard to justify (are these functions actually independent?), and unnecessary. The standard technique, from Kirsch and Mitzenmacher, is DOUBLE HASHING: compute just TWO base hash values h1 and h2 from the element — in practice one call to a good 128-bit non-cryptographic hash such as MurmurHash3 or xxHash, split into two 64-bit halves — and derive the i-th index as g_i(x) = (h1 + i * h2) mod m. Their result is that this yields the same asymptotic false-positive rate as k truly independent hashes, so you get k indices for the price of one hash computation. Three implementation notes that separate working code from subtly broken code: the intermediate h1 + i*h2 can overflow and go negative in Java, so use Math.floorMod or mask off the sign bit before the modulo, or a negative array index will throw at some unlucky input; if m is a power of two you can mask instead of dividing, but then h2 should be forced odd so the step size is coprime with m and the k indices do not collide with each other; and the hash must be seeded consistently across processes if filters are ever serialised and shared, otherwise two nodes disagree about what the bits mean. FOLLOW-UPS to expect. 'What if the real element count exceeds n?' — the false-positive rate rises per the formula, gracefully at first then sharply as the array saturates; monitor currentFpp and rebuild, or use a scalable filter that chains new sub-filters. 'Can two filters be merged?' — yes, bitwise OR, but ONLY if m, k and the hash seeds are identical; this is what makes filters map-reducible across shards, a genuinely useful property. 'How do you support deletion?' — counting filter, rebuild, or cuckoo filter, with the counter-overflow caveat. 'What about concurrency?' — add() only sets bits and setting a bit is idempotent, so a Bloom filter is remarkably concurrency-friendly: atomic OR on the word, or even racy plain writes with a slight risk of a lost update; the more careful answer uses an AtomicLongArray with getAndUpdate, or accepts that a lost set means a possible false negative and is therefore NOT acceptable when the guarantee matters. 'Alternatives?' — cuckoo filter (supports delete, smaller at low p), quotient filter (better locality, mergeable), and HyperLogLog for the different question of COUNTING distinct elements rather than testing membership; knowing the neighbourhood shows you chose Bloom rather than defaulted to it."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "SPACE vs ACCURACY is the dial, and it is explicit and continuous: about 4.8 extra bits per element per additional factor of ten in accuracy, so the design conversation is 'how much RAM do I have and how expensive is a false positive'. When a false positive costs a disk seek, 1 percent is generous; when it costs a cross-datacentre RPC, buy the extra bits. BLOOM vs HASH SET: the set is exact and supports deletion and enumeration but costs tens of bytes per element and scales with key length; the filter costs ~10 bits per element regardless of key length but is approximate, one-way and amnesiac. The decision rule is simply whether an authoritative store exists behind the filter — if not, you cannot use one, because there is nothing to resolve a 'maybe' against. BLOOM vs CUCKOO FILTER: cuckoo supports deletion, has better lookup locality (two cache lines, not k random probes), and is smaller for p below roughly 3 percent, but it can FAIL TO INSERT when its tables fill, which is an availability failure a Bloom filter never has; that insert-failure mode is usually the deciding factor. COUNTING variant: 4x memory for deletability plus an overflow hazard — usually the wrong trade compared with periodic rebuild. WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE: an under-sized filter degrades silently, which is the operational trap — always export currentFpp and the inserted count as metrics, and alert before saturation; a filter shared across processes breaks silently if hash seeds or parameters drift, so serialise the parameters WITH the bits and validate on load. EXTENSIONS worth naming: SCALABLE Bloom filters chain sub-filters with geometrically tightening error rates when n is unknown in advance; PARTITIONED filters give each hash function its own m/k-bit slice, improving cache behaviour and making the analysis cleaner; STABLE Bloom filters evict randomly to handle unbounded streams with bounded memory; and COMPRESSED filters exploit the fact that a filter sized for transmission rather than for querying should be built at a different k, since the optimal k for compressed size differs from the optimal k for in-memory query rate — a detail that reliably impresses when the interviewer knows it."),
    ],
    "The Bloom filter is the canonical probabilistic-structure interview, and it separates candidates on three axes. First, the CONTRACT: can you state that the error is one-sided, that false negatives are impossible because bits are never cleared, and that this is precisely what makes the filter safe as a pre-filter in front of an expensive lookup? Second, the MATHEMATICS as an engineering tool: p ~ (1 - e^(-kn/m))^k with optimal k = (m/n) ln 2 and m = -n ln p / (ln 2)^2, used to actually size a filter for a target error rate and to say '9.6 bits per element for 1 percent' without hesitation. Third, the DELETION question, which is the standard follow-up: explaining why clearing bits would create false negatives through bit sharing, and knowing that counting filters, rebuild-on-compaction, and cuckoo filters are the three real answers. Candidates who also mention double hashing to derive k indices from two base hashes signal they have implemented one rather than read about one.",
    [
      G("two_sided_error", "Describing the filter as merely approximate, or claiming it can produce false negatives as well as false positives.", "State the invariant: bits are only ever set, never cleared, so every bit an added element set is still set — mightContain can never return false for a member. The error is ONE-SIDED, which is exactly why a negative answer can be trusted to skip an expensive lookup.", "explaining the structure as 'sometimes wrong in both directions'; using the filter as the authoritative answer with no backing store; treating a true result as proof of membership."),
      G("guessed_sizing", "Choosing the bit-array size and the number of hash functions by feel — a round number of bits, three hash functions because three sounds independent.", "Derive both from requirements: m = -(n ln p) / (ln 2)^2 and k = (m/n) ln 2, then sanity-check with the standard figures (about 9.6 bits per element for p = 1 percent with k = 7); expose currentFpp so drift past the design n is observable.", "m picked as a power of ten with no reference to n or p; k fixed at 3 regardless of m/n; no answer to 'what happens when you insert twice as many elements as planned'."),
      G("delete_by_clearing", "Implementing remove(x) by clearing the k bits that x hashes to.", "Bits are shared between elements, so clearing them can un-set a bit another member depends on, producing a FALSE NEGATIVE and destroying the only guarantee the structure offers; use a counting Bloom filter (4x memory, watch counter saturation), rebuild the filter from the source data, or switch to a cuckoo filter which supports deletion natively.", "a remove() method that ANDs out bits; membership suddenly returning false for keys that exist; no discussion of counting variants when deletion is required."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.bloom, "Bloom filter class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── GRAPH ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m17", "lld_m17_t6", 6, "Design a Graph", "design-graph-adt",
    ["case-study", "data-structure", "api-design", "graph"],
    "One team needs a road network — 2 million intersections, each connected to four others, shortest paths run constantly. Another needs a 5,000-user social graph where the hot question is 'are A and B friends?' answered a million times a second. Both ask you for 'a Graph class'. Ship one API for both, and be ready to explain why the storage underneath is not the same.",
    "The design act in a graph ADT is not writing BFS — it is CHOOSING THE REPRESENTATION, and that choice is a function of density and of the operation mix, not of taste. An adjacency list costs O(V + E) memory and answers neighbours(v) in O(deg v) but hasEdge(u, v) in O(deg u); an adjacency matrix costs O(V^2) and answers hasEdge in O(1) but forces every neighbour scan to walk V slots. The road network is sparse and iterates neighbours, so it wants a list; the small dense social graph asks the edge-existence question constantly, so a bitset matrix at 5,000 vertices is 3 MB and answers in a single cache line. The API's job is to make that swappable: expose neighbours(v) as the ONE primitive, keep direction and weight behind the abstraction rather than behind boolean flags, and let algorithms consume an iterator so they never learn which storage they are running on.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a reusable GRAPH abstract data type over caller-supplied vertex objects. Structure operations: addVertex(v), removeVertex(v), addEdge(u, v) with an optional weight, removeEdge(u, v), hasEdge(u, v), neighbours(v), degree(v), vertexCount() and edgeCount(). Query operations that everything else is built on: iteration over vertices, iteration over the outgoing edges of a vertex, and — for directed graphs — access to incoming edges too, which is a requirement people forget until they need reverse reachability or in-degree for a topological sort. The same type must serve four combinations: DIRECTED or UNDIRECTED, WEIGHTED or UNWEIGHTED, and doing that without a constructor full of boolean flags is an explicit requirement, not a nicety. Traversals (BFS, DFS) and algorithms (shortest path, cycle detection, topological sort, connected components) should be able to run over the graph WITHOUT knowing how it is stored. Non-functional constraints that drive the whole design: MEMORY must be proportional to the graph's actual content for sparse graphs, since a 2-million-vertex road network cannot afford a V-by-V matrix (4 trillion cells); NEIGHBOUR ITERATION is the innermost loop of essentially every graph algorithm, so it must be allocation-free and cache-friendly; and hasEdge(u, v) must be fast in the workloads that need it, which is a DIFFERENT requirement that the same representation cannot serve equally well. Clarify with the interviewer: are self-loops and parallel edges allowed (usually not; say so and enforce it), is the graph mutable after construction, and roughly what V and E look like — that last question is the one that unlocks the representation decision, and asking it is itself the signal."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "GRAPH<V> is the public abstraction — an interface, deliberately, because the whole design rests on having more than one implementation behind it. Its contract is the operation list above, and the load-bearing method is neighbours(v), which returns an Iterable of edges or of adjacent vertices; every algorithm is written against that method and nothing else. EDGE is a small value object carrying from, to and weight; it exists so that weighted and unweighted graphs share one shape (unweighted edges simply carry weight 1, or the weight accessor is not consulted) instead of forking the type hierarchy. VERTEXINDEX is the quiet workhorse: algorithms want dense integer ids so they can use arrays for distances, visited flags and parents, while callers want to work with their own domain objects. A bidirectional map from V to an int id, assigned on first insertion, plus a reverse array from id to V, gives both — and it is also the piece that makes the two representations interchangeable, since a matrix needs integer indices by construction. ADJACENCYLISTGRAPH implements Graph with a Map from vertex to a List (or an int-keyed array of lists) of outgoing edges, and for directed graphs an optional parallel structure of incoming edges. ADJACENCYMATRIXGRAPH implements the same interface with a two-dimensional array — a long[][] of bitsets for unweighted graphs, a double[][] for weighted ones with a sentinel for absence. TRAVERSAL is a separate collaborator, not a method on Graph: BFS and DFS take a Graph plus a start vertex and either return an iterator or accept a visitor, which keeps algorithms out of the data structure. Notice the responsibility line: Graph stores and answers structural questions; Traversal and the algorithm classes compute. Merging them is the most common design mistake in this problem."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "Three design decisions carry this interview. FIRST, THE INTERFACE-PLUS-TWO-IMPLEMENTATIONS split. This is the rare case where an interface with more than one implementation is genuinely justified — the representations have identical semantics and wildly different performance profiles, so the choice must be deferrable to the caller who knows V, E and the query mix. Provide a factory that takes those hints (Graph.create(expectedVertices, expectedEdges) picking matrix when E approaches V^2 / 4 and V is small, list otherwise) so the common case needs no expertise, while an expert can construct either directly. SECOND, DIRECTEDNESS AND WEIGHT WITHOUT FLAG SOUP. The naive design is one class with booleans directed and weighted, and every method littered with if (directed) branches — which multiplies untested paths and makes 'undirected weighted' a configuration rather than a type. The clean approach is that UNDIRECTED IS A BEHAVIOUR, not a flag consulted at query time: addEdge on an undirected graph inserts BOTH directions once, at write time, so every read path is uniform and there is no branch in the hot loop. Weight is handled by always having an Edge with a weight field, defaulted to 1 for unweighted graphs, so Dijkstra and BFS read the same structure. If the type system should express the difference, separate DirectedGraph and UndirectedGraph types sharing a common Graph interface is the honest modelling — and knowing when a flag is acceptable (a single implementation detail that never branches a hot path) versus when it is a smell (behaviour selection scattered through methods) is exactly the judgment being probed. THIRD, ALGORITHMS DECOUPLED FROM STORAGE. Expose traversal as an ITERATOR (a BfsIterator implementing Iterator<V>) or as a VISITOR callback, and write every algorithm against Graph.neighbours. The payoff is concrete: swap adjacency list for matrix and not one algorithm changes; write a lazy graph whose neighbours are computed on demand (a chess move generator, a maze) and the same BFS finds the shortest path with no adaptation. That is the reuse the interviewer wants to see, and it is why neighbours(v) rather than getAdjacencyMatrix() must be the public primitive."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "interface Graph<V> {\n  void addVertex(V v);\n  void addEdge(V from, V to, double weight);\n  boolean removeEdge(V from, V to);\n  boolean hasEdge(V from, V to);\n  Iterable<Edge<V>> neighbours(V v);      // THE primitive every algorithm uses\n  Collection<V> vertices();\n  int vertexCount();\n  int edgeCount();\n}\n\nrecord Edge<V>(V from, V to, double weight) { }\n\n/** Sparse graphs: O(V + E) memory, O(deg v) neighbour scan, O(deg u) hasEdge. */\nclass AdjacencyListGraph<V> implements Graph<V> {\n  private final Map<V, List<Edge<V>>> out = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n  private final boolean undirected;\n  private int edges = 0;\n\n  AdjacencyListGraph(boolean undirected) { this.undirected = undirected; }\n\n  public void addVertex(V v) { out.computeIfAbsent(v, x -> new ArrayList<>()); }\n\n  public void addEdge(V from, V to, double w) {\n    addVertex(from); addVertex(to);\n    out.get(from).add(new Edge<>(from, to, w));\n    if (undirected) out.get(to).add(new Edge<>(to, from, w));  // symmetry at WRITE\n    edges++;                                                   // time, not read time\n  }\n\n  public boolean removeEdge(V from, V to) {\n    List<Edge<V>> l = out.get(from);\n    if (l == null || !l.removeIf(e -> e.to().equals(to))) return false;\n    if (undirected) out.get(to).removeIf(e -> e.to().equals(from));\n    edges--; return true;                       // O(deg) — the list's weak spot\n  }\n\n  public boolean hasEdge(V from, V to) {        // O(deg from): the trade-off\n    List<Edge<V>> l = out.get(from);\n    if (l == null) return false;\n    for (Edge<V> e : l) if (e.to().equals(to)) return true;\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  public Iterable<Edge<V>> neighbours(V v) { return out.getOrDefault(v, List.of()); }\n  public Collection<V> vertices() { return out.keySet(); }\n  public int vertexCount() { return out.size(); }\n  public int edgeCount() { return edges; }\n}\n\n/** Dense/small graphs: O(V^2) bits, O(1) hasEdge, O(V) neighbour scan. */\nclass AdjacencyMatrixGraph<V> implements Graph<V> {\n  private final VertexIndex<V> index;           // V <-> dense int id\n  private final long[][] bits;                  // row-major bitset\n  // hasEdge: (bits[i][j >>> 6] & (1L << j)) != 0        -> one cache line\n  // neighbours: scan row i, yielding set bits            -> O(V) even if deg = 2\n}\n\n/** Algorithms depend on neighbours() ONLY, so storage can be swapped freely. */\nclass Traversals {\n  static <V> List<V> bfs(Graph<V> g, V start) {\n    List<V> order = new ArrayList<>();\n    Set<V> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    Deque<V> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    queue.add(start); seen.add(start);\n    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n      V v = queue.poll();\n      order.add(v);\n      for (Edge<V> e : g.neighbours(v))         // the only coupling point\n        if (seen.add(e.to())) queue.add(e.to());\n    }\n    return order;                                // O(V + E) on a list graph\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the representation decision — list vs matrix vs edge list, by density and query mix",
        "This is the design act, so make the analysis quantitative rather than qualitative. ADJACENCY LIST stores, for each vertex, a collection of its outgoing edges. Memory is O(V + E) — in Java, one map entry plus one list object plus E edge objects, which in practice is 40 to 60 bytes per edge unless you use primitive int arrays, a real consideration at scale. neighbours(v) is O(deg v) and, better, it touches only the memory that is actually relevant, so it is the representation every traversal wants. hasEdge(u, v) is O(deg u) — a linear scan of one list — which is fine when degrees are small and terrible when a hub vertex has a million neighbours; storing each adjacency as a HashSet instead of a List makes hasEdge expected O(1) at the cost of memory and of losing edge ordering, and that sub-decision is worth naming. removeEdge is also O(deg u). ADJACENCY MATRIX stores a V-by-V grid. hasEdge and removeEdge are O(1), which is unbeatable, and for an unweighted graph a bitset row packs 64 edges per word so a 5,000-vertex graph costs 5,000 * 5,000 / 8 bytes ~ 3 MB and each edge test is a single memory access. The costs are severe for sparse graphs: memory is O(V^2) regardless of how few edges exist, and neighbours(v) must scan V entries even to find two neighbours, which turns an O(V + E) BFS into O(V^2). For the 2-million-vertex road network the matrix is 4 * 10^12 cells — not a slow choice, an impossible one. EDGE LIST is the third representation and gets forgotten: just a flat list of edges, O(E) memory, terrible for neighbour queries (O(E) scan) but ideal for algorithms that consume edges globally — Kruskal's minimum spanning tree sorts all edges, Bellman-Ford relaxes all edges V-1 times — and it is the natural format for input, serialisation and streaming. THE DECISION RULE, said crisply: let density d = E / V^2. If d is small (sparse — most real graphs: roads, social networks, dependency graphs, the web, all with average degree in the single or low double digits) use an adjacency list. If the graph is dense, E approaching V^2, or if V is small enough that V^2 is cheap AND hasEdge dominates the workload, use a matrix. If the algorithm consumes edges globally rather than per-vertex, use an edge list. Then the second-order refinement that impresses: for large static sparse graphs, neither Java structure is really right — a COMPRESSED SPARSE ROW layout (one int array of edge targets, one offsets array of length V+1) gives adjacency-list asymptotics with matrix-like memory density and perfect cache locality for scans, and it is what every serious graph library uses. Mentioning CSR signals you have worked with real graphs and not just interview-sized ones."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: one API over directed/undirected and weighted/unweighted, without flag soup",
        "The requirement to serve four combinations is where the object design gets tested, and the failure mode is predictable: a Graph class with booleans directed and weighted, and then if (directed) branches scattered through addEdge, removeEdge, degree, and every algorithm that must remember to ask. Each flag doubles the paths through the class, most of which are never tested together, and the flags leak into callers who start writing if (graph.isDirected()) themselves. The design principle to state is that CONFIGURATION SHOULD BE RESOLVED AT WRITE TIME OR IN THE TYPE, NEVER RE-EXAMINED ON EVERY READ. For UNDIRECTEDNESS the resolution is elegant: an undirected edge is simply two directed edges inserted together. addEdge(u, v) on an undirected graph appends v to u's list and u to v's list, and after that every read path — neighbours, degree, BFS, connected components — is identical to the directed case with zero branches in the hot loop. The only places that still need to know are edgeCount (halve it, or track a separate counter as the code does) and removeEdge (remove both halves), which is two methods rather than twenty. For WEIGHT the resolution is to always carry an Edge object with a weight field, defaulting to 1.0 when unweighted. BFS ignores the field; Dijkstra reads it; nothing forks. The alternative — separate WeightedEdge and UnweightedEdge types — forces generic gymnastics and duplicate algorithm implementations for no behavioural gain. If the interviewer pushes on type safety, the honest upgrade is separate DirectedGraph and UndirectedGraph interfaces extending a common Graph, so a method that requires directionality (topological sort) can demand DirectedGraph in its signature and be unable to receive the wrong thing — that is a compile-time guarantee a boolean can never give, and it is the right answer when the graph type is a library boundary. State the general rule you are applying, because it generalises well beyond graphs: a boolean parameter that selects BEHAVIOUR is a smell (make it a type, or resolve it at construction); a boolean that records a FACT the class merely stores is fine. Finally, the ergonomics layer: provide addEdge(u, v) that defaults weight to 1, let addEdge auto-create missing vertices (with a documented decision — the alternative, throwing on unknown vertices, catches typos but makes bulk loading tedious), and reject self-loops and parallel edges explicitly unless the domain needs them, because silently allowing them breaks algorithms that assume simple graphs."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: mutation costs, index invalidation, and the follow-ups",
        "MUTABILITY IS WHERE GRAPH DESIGNS BREAK, and interviewers probe it because it is the part nobody rehearses. EDGE REMOVAL: on an adjacency list backed by ArrayList it is O(deg u) to find plus O(deg u) to shift, and on an undirected graph you pay it twice; switching adjacency to a LinkedHashSet makes it expected O(1) with a memory and locality penalty. On a matrix it is a single bit clear — one of the few places the matrix simply wins. VERTEX REMOVAL is the genuinely nasty one and the follow-up to be ready for. On an adjacency list, removing vertex v means dropping v's own list (easy) and removing every edge POINTING AT v (hard): without incoming-edge tracking you must scan every other vertex's list, O(V + E). Keeping a reverse adjacency structure for directed graphs makes it O(deg in + deg out) but doubles the write cost and the memory, and is the right trade only when reverse queries or vertex deletion are common — say which workload you are optimising for. On a matrix, vertex removal is worse in a subtle way: the vertex has a dense integer index, and removing it either leaves a HOLE (a tombstone row and column, wasting memory and requiring every iteration to skip dead indices) or triggers a COMPACTION that renumbers vertices — which INVALIDATES every id that any algorithm, cached result, or client currently holds. That invalidation is the real design hazard: any external structure keyed by vertex index (a distance array, a saved path, a UI selection) becomes silently wrong rather than loudly broken. The standard resolutions are to make ids stable forever and tolerate holes with a generation counter for reuse, to expose only the vertex objects publicly and keep ids strictly internal, or to declare the graph IMMUTABLE after construction with a builder for the loading phase — which is what most high-performance libraries do, because immutability also enables the CSR layout, safe concurrent reads with no locking, and cacheable derived data. CONCURRENT MODIFICATION deserves a line too: a traversal holding an iterator over an adjacency list while another thread adds an edge is a ConcurrentModificationException at best and a corrupted traversal at worst, so either the graph is immutable during algorithms, or it is snapshotted, or reads take a lock. FOLLOW-UPS: 'Add k-shortest-paths / Dijkstra' — it is an algorithm class consuming neighbours plus a priority queue, no change to the graph. 'A billion edges?' — CSR with primitive arrays, or memory-mapped files, or partitioned across machines with the LLD giving way to distributed graph processing. 'Support multigraphs?' — the adjacency value becomes a list of parallel edges with ids, and hasEdge splits into hasEdge and edgesBetween. 'Serialise it?' — edge list, because it is the representation-independent format."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "LIST vs MATRIX vs EDGE LIST, restated as a decision table you can say out loud: adjacency list is O(V + E) memory, O(deg) neighbour scan, O(deg) edge test — the default for sparse graphs, which is nearly all real graphs; adjacency matrix is O(V^2) memory, O(V) neighbour scan, O(1) edge test — correct for dense or small graphs and for edge-test-dominated workloads; edge list is O(E) memory, poor per-vertex queries, ideal for global-edge algorithms and for serialisation. The reason the interface exists is that this choice must be reversible without touching algorithms. HASHSET vs ARRAYLIST ADJACENCY: the set buys expected O(1) hasEdge and removeEdge at the cost of roughly 3x memory per edge and worse iteration locality — worth it exactly when edge existence is queried hot, which is the same question the matrix answers, so consider both. MUTABLE vs IMMUTABLE: an immutable graph built through a builder enables CSR packing, lock-free concurrent reads, safe caching of derived results, and stable ids forever; a mutable graph is necessary for live systems (a social graph, a live dependency tracker) and pays for it with iterator-invalidation rules and index-management policy. Most libraries offer both and so should your answer. GENERIC VERTEX OBJECTS vs INT IDS: generics give a pleasant API but every neighbour lookup goes through hashCode and equals on the vertex object, which is measurably slower and requires those methods to be correct and immutable — a vertex whose hashCode changes after insertion corrupts the map exactly as it would in a HashMap, and that is a real bug worth naming. The dense-int-id layer under a generic surface is the standard compromise. EXTENSION POINTS: attach vertex and edge PROPERTIES via side maps rather than by subclassing Edge, so the storage stays uniform; support LAZY or IMPLICIT graphs (a chess position generator, a maze, a state space) by implementing neighbours() computationally with no stored edges at all — the strongest proof that the abstraction is right, since BFS then finds a checkmate path with zero changes; and expose a reversed() VIEW rather than a copy for directed graphs, since reverse reachability is a common need and a view costs nothing."),
    ],
    "Design-a-Graph is misread by most candidates as an algorithms question and answered with BFS on a HashMap of lists. The interviewer is testing something else: whether you treat the REPRESENTATION as the design decision and can justify adjacency list versus matrix versus edge list from density and the operation mix, with the memory and edge-existence consequences quantified rather than hand-waved. The second axis is API design — serving directed/undirected and weighted/unweighted from one abstraction without a boolean-flag mess, by resolving undirectedness at write time and always carrying a weight field, or by splitting the types. The third is decoupling: algorithms written against neighbours(v) alone, exposed as an iterator or visitor, so storage can be swapped and even implicit graphs work. Follow-ups almost always land on mutation — edge-removal cost, and vertex deletion invalidating dense indices — which is where a rehearsed answer runs out and a designed one keeps going.",
    [
      G("representation_unjustified", "Reaching straight for a Map of vertex to list (or, worse, a matrix) without asking about V, E and the query mix, and never mentioning the alternative.", "Treat the representation as THE design decision: adjacency list for sparse graphs at O(V + E) memory with O(deg) edge tests, matrix for dense or small graphs at O(V^2) memory with O(1) edge tests, edge list for global-edge algorithms — and mention CSR for large static graphs.", "no question about graph size or density before coding; a matrix proposed for a million-vertex graph; hasEdge presented as O(1) on an adjacency list."),
      G("boolean_flag_soup", "A single Graph class with directed and weighted boolean fields, branching on them inside addEdge, removeEdge, degree and the algorithms.", "Resolve configuration at write time or in the type: an undirected addEdge inserts both directions once so every read path is branch-free, weight is always an Edge field defaulting to 1, and if compile-time safety matters split DirectedGraph and UndirectedGraph over a shared interface.", "if (directed) appearing in more than one method; callers calling graph.isDirected(); separate near-duplicate algorithm implementations for the weighted and unweighted cases."),
      G("algorithms_fused_to_storage", "Putting BFS, DFS and shortest-path methods on the Graph class itself, or writing algorithms against the adjacency map directly.", "Make neighbours(v) the single primitive and keep traversal in separate iterator or visitor collaborators, so the storage can be swapped, and so an implicit graph (a move generator, a maze) works with the same algorithms and no stored edges.", "algorithms reading the internal Map or matrix field; getAdjacencyList() exposed publicly; changing the representation requiring edits to every algorithm."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.graph, "Graph ADT class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── BALANCED BST (AVL / RED-BLACK) ───────────────────────
  TT("lld_m17", "lld_m17_t7", 7, "Design a Balanced BST (AVL / Red-Black)", "design-balanced-bst",
    ["case-study", "data-structure", "trees", "invariants"],
    "You ship a binary search tree, tests pass, benchmarks look great. Then a customer loads their data — already sorted by id, as exports usually are — and every lookup slows to a crawl. The tree is a linked list a million nodes long. What invariant were you missing, what is the ONE operation that restores it, and why do the standard libraries choose red-black over AVL when AVL gives faster lookups?",
    "A plain BST has no invariant tying its height to n, so sorted input degenerates it to O(n). Balanced trees add one — AVL bounds the height difference of every node's subtrees to 1; red-black instead colours nodes so no path is more than twice as long as any other — and both restore it with the SAME primitive: ROTATION, a local O(1) pointer swap that reduces height while preserving the in-order key sequence. The two invariants trade against each other in exactly one way, and it explains the library choice: AVL is more rigidly balanced, so it is shorter and reads are faster; red-black tolerates more imbalance, so it does far fewer rotations and writes are faster — which is why TreeMap, std::map and the Linux kernel pick red-black.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: an ORDERED MAP or set over keys with a total order, offering get(key), put(key, value) with replacement on duplicates, remove(key), containsKey, size, and the order-dependent operations that justify a tree over a hash map: min(), max(), floor(key), ceiling(key), predecessor and successor, IN-ORDER ITERATION producing keys in sorted sequence, and RANGE queries returning all keys in an interval. Keys are ordered by Comparable or an injected Comparator; nulls are rejected because they cannot be ordered; the comparator must be consistent with equals or the structure's notion of duplicate keys diverges from the caller's. Non-functional requirements are the entire content of the problem: every operation must be O(log n) in the WORST CASE, not on average and not on lucky inputs — specifically, the structure must be immune to adversarial or merely natural insertion orders, since sorted input is the common case in real systems (database exports, timestamps, auto-increment ids) and is precisely what destroys a naive BST. Memory overhead per node must be small and constant — a couple of child pointers plus either a height byte (AVL) or a single colour bit (red-black). Iteration must not require recursion proportional to n on the call stack for a deep tree, and the structure should support efficient bulk construction from sorted input, which can be done in O(n) rather than n insertions at O(log n) each. Clarify: duplicates replaced rather than stored, no concurrency in the first pass (say explicitly that a concurrent balanced tree is a much harder problem and why), and whether the tree is a map or a multiset."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "NODE is the whole data model, and its fields ARE the invariant, so name them deliberately. Every version has key, value, left and right. An AVL node additionally stores HEIGHT (or, equivalently, a balance factor in [-1, 1]) — an int, updated on the way back up from every insert and delete, because the invariant is arithmetic about subtree heights and cannot be checked without it. A red-black node instead stores a single COLOUR bit, red or black — one bit against AVL's byte or int, which is a real memory difference at scale — because the red-black invariant is expressed in colours rather than in measured heights. A PARENT pointer is optional and is a genuine design decision: it makes successor, predecessor and iteration easy and non-recursive, and it makes deletion fix-up loops natural, but it costs a pointer per node and one more field to keep consistent through every rotation, which is one more place to introduce a bug; the alternative is recursion (or an explicit stack in the iterator) that carries the path implicitly. Real implementations differ — the JDK's TreeMap keeps parent pointers, many textbook AVLs do not. COMPARATOR holds the ordering. The TREE object owns the root, the size, the comparator, and — this is the responsibility that matters — the BALANCE MAINTENANCE, which no external code may bypass; there is no public method that hands out a Node, because any external mutation breaks the invariant silently. ROTATION is not a class but it is the design's single primitive, and factoring rotateLeft and rotateRight as private helpers that every fix-up case calls is the difference between a readable implementation and the sprawling case analysis people usually produce. A NIL SENTINEL is worth mentioning for red-black: using a shared black sentinel node instead of null removes a mountain of null checks from the fix-up code."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "Start by naming WHY balance is needed at all, because the interviewer wants the failure before the fix. A binary search tree maintains only the ORDERING invariant — everything in the left subtree is smaller, everything in the right is larger — and that invariant says nothing about SHAPE. Insert 1, 2, 3, ..., n in order and every new key goes to the right of everything before it: the tree is a right spine, height n - 1, and get() is a linear scan through n nodes with terrible cache behaviour on top. The bug is not rare or adversarial; sorted input is the normal shape of real data. So a second invariant is needed, one that BOUNDS THE HEIGHT relative to n, and the design question is which one. AVL's invariant is height-based and strict: for every node, |height(left) - height(right)| <= 1, which forces height <= 1.44 * log2(n). RED-BLACK's invariant is colour-based and looser: the root is black, red nodes have black children, and every root-to-leaf path contains the same number of black nodes — from which it follows that the longest path is at most twice the shortest, so height <= 2 * log2(n + 1). Both are O(log n); the constants differ, and the consequence is the design's central trade-off, developed in the deep dive below. The structural design is the same for both: a private Node type, all mutation funnelled through put and remove, and rebalancing implemented as ROTATIONS applied along the path from the modified node back toward the root. The single most valuable design instinct here is recognising that ROTATION IS THE ONLY PRIMITIVE — the four textbook cases (LL, LR, RL, RR) are not four algorithms but two rotations composed, and the colour or height bookkeeping is the rest. Deliberately absent patterns: no Strategy for the balancing scheme (a tree is AVL or red-black at construction and the invariant permeates every method — pretending it is pluggable is a fantasy), and no exposed Node type. What DOES belong: an iterator implemented with an explicit stack (or parent pointers) so iteration is O(1) space per level and never blows the call stack, and a validate() method used only in tests that asserts the invariant across the whole tree — the audit that catches the bug rotations always eventually introduce."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java) — AVL insert with the four rotation cases",
        "class AvlTree<K extends Comparable<K>, V> {\n  static class Node<K, V> {\n    K key; V value; Node<K, V> left, right;\n    int height = 1;                       // leaf height = 1; null height = 0\n    Node(K k, V v) { key = k; value = v; }\n  }\n\n  private Node<K, V> root;\n  private int size;\n\n  private static int h(Node<?, ?> n) { return n == null ? 0 : n.height; }\n  private static void recompute(Node<?, ?> n) { n.height = 1 + Math.max(h(n.left), h(n.right)); }\n  private static int balance(Node<?, ?> n) { return n == null ? 0 : h(n.left) - h(n.right); }\n\n  /** Rotation: O(1) pointer swap that lowers height and PRESERVES in-order order. */\n  private Node<K, V> rotateRight(Node<K, V> y) {\n    Node<K, V> x = y.left, t = x.right;\n    x.right = y; y.left = t;              // y's left subtree's right child moves to y\n    recompute(y); recompute(x);           // order matters: child before new parent\n    return x;                             // x is the new subtree root\n  }\n\n  private Node<K, V> rotateLeft(Node<K, V> x) {\n    Node<K, V> y = x.right, t = y.left;\n    y.left = x; x.right = t;\n    recompute(x); recompute(y);\n    return y;\n  }\n\n  public void put(K key, V value) { root = insert(root, key, value); }\n\n  private Node<K, V> insert(Node<K, V> n, K key, V value) {\n    if (n == null) { size++; return new Node<>(key, value); }\n    int c = key.compareTo(n.key);\n    if (c < 0)      n.left  = insert(n.left, key, value);\n    else if (c > 0) n.right = insert(n.right, key, value);\n    else { n.value = value; return n; }   // duplicate: replace, no rebalance\n\n    recompute(n);\n    int b = balance(n);\n    // FOUR CASES — but only TWO primitives, composed:\n    if (b > 1 && key.compareTo(n.left.key)  < 0) return rotateRight(n);          // LL\n    if (b < -1 && key.compareTo(n.right.key) > 0) return rotateLeft(n);          // RR\n    if (b > 1) { n.left = rotateLeft(n.left);   return rotateRight(n); }         // LR\n    if (b < -1) { n.right = rotateRight(n.right); return rotateLeft(n); }        // RL\n    return n;                              // insert needs AT MOST ONE fix (2 rotations)\n  }\n\n  public V get(K key) {\n    Node<K, V> n = root;\n    while (n != null) {\n      int c = key.compareTo(n.key);\n      if (c == 0) return n.value;\n      n = c < 0 ? n.left : n.right;        // height <= 1.44 log2 n, so O(log n) worst case\n    }\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  private Node<K, V> remove(Node<K, V> n, K key) {\n    if (n == null) return null;\n    int c = key.compareTo(n.key);\n    if (c < 0)      n.left  = remove(n.left, key);\n    else if (c > 0) n.right = remove(n.right, key);\n    else {\n      if (n.left == null || n.right == null) { size--; return n.left != null ? n.left : n.right; }\n      Node<K, V> s = n.right;              // two children: take in-order successor\n      while (s.left != null) s = s.left;\n      n.key = s.key; n.value = s.value;    // copy, then delete the successor node\n      n.right = remove(n.right, s.key);\n    }\n    recompute(n);\n    int b = balance(n);                    // rebalancing here can CASCADE upward,\n    if (b > 1  && balance(n.left)  >= 0) return rotateRight(n);   // unlike insert\n    if (b > 1) { n.left = rotateLeft(n.left); return rotateRight(n); }\n    if (b < -1 && balance(n.right) <= 0) return rotateLeft(n);\n    if (b < -1) { n.right = rotateRight(n.right); return rotateLeft(n); }\n    return n;\n  }\n\n  /** Test-only audit: the invariant must hold at EVERY node. */\n  boolean isBalanced(Node<K, V> n) {\n    if (n == null) return true;\n    return Math.abs(balance(n)) <= 1 && isBalanced(n.left) && isBalanced(n.right);\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: rotation, the single primitive — and why four cases are really two",
        "Everything a balanced tree does to fix itself is a ROTATION, so understand it exactly rather than memorising diagrams. Consider node y with left child x. A RIGHT ROTATION makes x the subtree root: x's right subtree becomes y's left subtree, and y becomes x's right child. Three pointer assignments, constant time, no allocation, no traversal. The two properties that make it the right primitive are worth stating explicitly. FIRST, IT PRESERVES THE IN-ORDER SEQUENCE. Before the rotation the in-order walk is (x's left subtree, x, x's right subtree, y, y's right subtree); after it, the walk is (x's left subtree, x, [x's old right subtree now under y as y's left], y, y's right subtree) — identical. The search-tree ordering invariant survives untouched, which is why rotation is legal at all; a fix-up that reordered keys would be a different tree. SECOND, IT MOVES HEIGHT SIDEWAYS: the left subtree loses a level and the right gains one, which is exactly the corrective action needed when the left is too tall. Now the four cases. Imbalance is detected at a node z whose subtree heights differ by more than one, and it is classified by the direction of the two steps down toward the newly inserted key: LEFT-LEFT, LEFT-RIGHT, RIGHT-LEFT, RIGHT-RIGHT. LL and RR are the STRAIGHT-LINE cases and take one rotation — right for LL, left for RR. LR and RL are the ZIG-ZAG cases, and here is the insight: a single rotation does NOT fix them, because rotating a zig-zag simply produces the mirror zig-zag. The fix is to first rotate the CHILD in the opposite direction, converting the zig-zag into a straight line, then apply the straight-line rotation to the node itself. So there are two primitives and four cases, and LR = rotateLeft(child) then rotateRight(node), RL = rotateRight(child) then rotateLeft(node). Getting this articulated as composition rather than as four memorised recipes is the marker of real understanding. Two implementation details that bite everyone: heights must be recomputed BOTTOM-UP — the old subtree root before the new one, as the code does — or the new root's height is computed from a stale child; and the rotation helpers must RETURN the new subtree root and the caller must reassign the parent's child pointer (n.left = insert(n.left, ...)), because otherwise the rotation happens locally and is never spliced back in, a bug that produces a tree that is silently missing nodes. The same primitive powers red-black fix-up, splay trees, treaps and B-tree node splits' conceptual cousin — learn it once."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: AVL vs red-black — the rigidity trade, and why libraries chose red-black",
        "Both invariants give O(log n), so a candidate who stops at 'they are both logarithmic' has missed the question. The difference is HOW TIGHTLY each constrains the shape, and every practical consequence flows from that one dial. AVL is STRICT: |height(left) - height(right)| <= 1 at every node, which bounds the height at about 1.44 * log2(n). RED-BLACK is LOOSE: colour rules that only guarantee the longest path is at most twice the shortest, bounding height at about 2 * log2(n). Consequence one, READS: an AVL tree is shorter, so a lookup follows fewer pointers — roughly 30 percent fewer comparisons in the worst case — making AVL the better choice for read-dominated workloads such as an in-memory index that is built once and queried constantly. Consequence two, WRITES: maintaining a strict invariant costs more corrective work. Insertion is comparable in both (at most two rotations, so O(1) rotations either way), but DELETION diverges sharply — an AVL delete can require rotations at EVERY level on the path back to the root, up to O(log n) of them, whereas a red-black delete needs at most THREE rotations regardless of tree size, with the rest of the fix-up being recolouring, which is a cheap local write and not a pointer restructure. Consequence three, BOOKKEEPING: AVL stores a height (a byte or an int) and must recompute it up the entire path on every insert and delete, even when no rotation occurs; red-black stores one bit and often terminates its fix-up early. Put those together and the library choice explains itself: java.util.TreeMap, C++ std::map and std::set, the Linux kernel's rbtree for scheduler and memory-management structures, and most language runtimes use RED-BLACK, because general-purpose containers see mixed read/write workloads and cannot afford deletion to cascade, while the extra 30 percent of path length costs little in practice given that both are shallow and memory-bound anyway. AVL survives where reads truly dominate and writes are rare — some database index structures, and read-heavy caches. State the rule as a sentence: MORE RIGID BALANCE MEANS FASTER SEARCH AND SLOWER UPDATE; the two schemes sit at different points on that curve, and neither is 'better'. Two honourable mentions that show breadth: a TREAP gets expected O(log n) with radically simpler code by assigning each node a random priority and rotating to maintain a heap on priorities — the randomised cousin of this problem, and the same expected-versus-worst-case distinction as a skip list; and a B-TREE is what you actually use when nodes live on disk or when cache lines dominate, because the branching factor, not the balance scheme, is the lever that matters there."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: why deletion is the hard half, and the follow-ups to expect",
        "Insertion in a balanced tree is genuinely easy: the new node is a leaf, it can only make one path longer by one, and a SINGLE fix (one or two rotations) at the lowest unbalanced ancestor restores the invariant for the whole tree — the height of that subtree returns to what it was before the insert, so nothing above it can still be unbalanced. That last clause is the reason insert is bounded: THE FIX IS ABSORBING. Deletion has no such property, and that asymmetry is the follow-up interviewers reach for when they want to separate people who have implemented a tree from people who have read about one. Three difficulties compound. FIRST, THE STRUCTURAL CASE ANALYSIS. Deleting a node with zero or one child is a splice, but deleting a node with TWO children cannot remove the node at all — you find its IN-ORDER SUCCESSOR (the leftmost node of the right subtree, which by construction has no left child), copy its key and value into the doomed node, and then delete the successor instead, recursively. Getting this reduction right, and knowing why the successor is guaranteed to have at most one child, is table stakes. SECOND, THE CASCADE. Removing a node can SHORTEN a subtree, and a shortened subtree may unbalance its parent; fixing the parent by rotation can shorten THAT subtree, which may unbalance the grandparent — so in AVL the rebalancing propagates all the way to the root in the worst case, O(log n) rotations, versus at most two for insert. Red-black's looser invariant is what caps its deletion at three rotations plus recolouring, and this is precisely the trade the previous deep dive described, seen from the implementation side. THIRD, THE CASE EXPLOSION IN RED-BLACK. Deleting a black node breaks the equal-black-height rule and produces the 'double black' condition, whose resolution splits into the famous four cases depending on the sibling's colour and the sibling's children's colours — this is the code every textbook prints and nobody enjoys, and it is why using a shared NIL SENTINEL instead of null, and why writing a validate() invariant checker for tests, are not niceties but survival tactics. FOLLOW-UPS to expect: 'Why not just rebuild the tree when it gets unbalanced?' — a scapegoat tree does exactly that, achieving amortised O(log n) with no per-node balance data at all, at the price of occasional O(n) rebuild pauses — a legitimate answer with a latency profile you must own. 'Why a tree and not a hash map?' — ordered iteration, range queries, floor and ceiling, and worst-case rather than expected bounds; if none of those are needed, a hash map is faster and simpler. 'How do you make it concurrent?' — badly: rotations rewrite regions whose extent you cannot predict, which is exactly why concurrent ordered maps are skip lists; the practical answers are a read-write lock, a copy-on-write persistent tree for read-heavy workloads, or a B-tree with per-node latches. 'Build from sorted input?' — build the perfectly balanced tree directly in O(n) by recursive midpoint selection instead of n inserts."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "AVL vs RED-BLACK, stated as the decision rule: choose AVL for read-dominated workloads (about 1.44 log n height, roughly 30 percent shorter search paths, but deletion rebalancing can cascade O(log n) levels and every operation maintains a height field); choose RED-BLACK for mixed or write-heavy workloads (2 log n height, at most 2 rotations on insert and 3 on delete, one colour bit, cheap recolouring instead of restructuring) — which is why it is the standard-library default. BALANCED BST vs HASH MAP: the hash map wins on point lookups (expected O(1) versus O(log n)) and on constant factors; the tree wins on ordered iteration, range and neighbour queries, and on offering WORST-CASE rather than expected bounds, which matters when an adversary can influence keys. Do not present the tree as a slower hash map; present it as the structure whose API includes order. BALANCED BST vs B-TREE: once nodes are on disk or once cache misses dominate, the branching factor becomes the lever — a B-tree with 100 keys per node has depth 3 where a binary tree has depth 20, and each node fills a page or several cache lines. Every database index is a B+ tree for exactly this reason, and knowing that the binary structures are an in-memory, cache-resident choice is the systems awareness interviewers reward. BALANCED BST vs SKIP LIST: same expected performance, skip list far simpler to implement and to make concurrent, tree gives worst-case guarantees and lower memory. RANDOMISED ALTERNATIVES: treaps and randomised BSTs get expected O(log n) with dramatically less code and no deletion case explosion, trading the worst-case guarantee — the same bargain as a skip list. SCAPEGOAT TREES trade occasional O(n) rebuilds for zero per-node metadata. EXTENSIONS worth naming: augment each node with a SUBTREE SIZE to get O(log n) rank and select (the k-th smallest key) and O(log n) count-in-range, which is the standard order-statistic tree and a very common follow-up; augment with subtree MIN/MAX to build an interval tree for overlap queries; make the tree PERSISTENT by path-copying on write, giving immutable snapshots at O(log n) extra memory per update, which is how functional languages provide ordered maps and how you get lock-free readers for free."),
    ],
    "This is the interview where reciting AVL rotations is not enough. The first thing being tested is WHY: can you explain that a plain BST has no shape invariant, so ordinary sorted input — the most common shape of real data — degenerates it to a linked list with O(n) lookups? The second is the primitive: rotation as a local O(1) pointer swap that lowers height while preserving in-order order, with the four cases understood as compositions of two rotations rather than four memorised recipes. The third, and the one that separates senior answers, is the AVL-versus-red-black trade: AVL is more rigidly balanced so searches are faster, red-black tolerates more skew so updates are far cheaper — at most three rotations on delete against AVL's possible O(log n) cascade — which is exactly why TreeMap, std::map and the Linux kernel all chose red-black. Expect the follow-up on deletion (in-order successor, cascading rebalancing, the double-black cases) and on why concurrent ordered maps are skip lists instead.",
    [
      G("no_balance_rationale", "Presenting a balanced tree as O(log n) without being able to say what a plain BST does wrong, or claiming degenerate trees only occur on adversarial input.", "Lead with the failure: a BST enforces ordering but nothing about shape, so ascending input (ids, timestamps, sorted exports — the normal case) builds a right spine of height n and every lookup becomes an O(n) pointer chase; the balance invariant exists to bound height by O(log n) regardless of insertion order.", "cannot answer 'what happens if I insert 1..n in order'; describing BST operations as O(log n) with no worst-case caveat; no invariant stated at all."),
      G("rotations_as_four_recipes", "Memorising LL, LR, RL and RR as four separate procedures without seeing that there are two primitives, or writing rotations that forget to recompute heights bottom-up and to return the new subtree root.", "Frame rotation as ONE primitive: an O(1) pointer swap preserving the in-order sequence while moving a level from one side to the other; zig-zag cases are fixed by rotating the child first to straighten the line, then rotating the node — recompute the old root's height before the new root's, and always reassign the parent's child pointer from the rotation's return value.", "four independent blocks of pointer code with no shared helper; heights updated top-down or not at all; rotate() called without assigning its result back into n.left / n.right, silently dropping nodes."),
      G("avl_rb_equivalent", "Treating AVL and red-black as interchangeable because both are O(log n), with no account of why standard libraries picked red-black.", "Name the rigidity trade: AVL keeps |balance| <= 1 so height is about 1.44 log2 n and lookups are faster, while red-black allows the longest path to be twice the shortest (height about 2 log2 n) but needs at most 2 rotations on insert and 3 on delete versus AVL's potential O(log n) delete cascade — cheaper writes are why TreeMap, std::map and the kernel rbtree chose it.", "comparison table listing only the complexity class; claiming red-black is 'just a different way to do the same thing'; no mention that AVL deletion can rebalance at every level while insertion cannot."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.bst, "Balanced BST class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── HASH MAP ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m17", "lld_m17_t8", 8, "Design a Hash Map", "design-hash-map",
    ["case-study", "data-structure", "hashing", "amortized-analysis"],
    "Everyone says a hash map is O(1). Then a service starts timing out, and the profile shows one bucket holding 40,000 entries because an attacker sent 40,000 keys chosen to collide — or because someone wrote a hashCode that returns a constant. Design the hash map, and be precise: O(1) in what sense, resting on which assumption, and what happens the moment that assumption fails?",
    "A hash map is a bucket ARRAY plus a COLLISION STRATEGY plus a RESIZE POLICY, and every interesting property comes from those three. The bound is EXPECTED O(1), and the expectation is entirely over the quality of the hash: with a good hash and a bounded load factor the average chain is short, but a degenerate hash collapses a bucket into a linear scan, which is O(n) worst case and a real denial-of-service vector — Java 8 mitigates it by TREEIFYING long chains into red-black trees, capping the damage at O(log n). Resize is the other half: doubling and rehashing costs O(n), but because it happens after O(n) insertions the AMORTISED cost per put stays O(1) — with the caveat that amortised is not per-operation, so latency-sensitive systems rehash INCREMENTALLY instead.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a MAP from keys to values with put(key, value) replacing any existing mapping and returning the old value, get(key) returning the value or null, remove(key), containsKey, size, clear, and iteration over keys, values and entries. Null keys and null values are a policy decision that must be stated rather than defaulted — HashMap permits one null key and null values, ConcurrentHashMap permits neither because a null return then cannot distinguish absent from mapped-to-null; picking a side and giving that reason is the answer. Keys must implement a consistent equals and hashCode; the map's contract depends on it and the map cannot enforce it, which is itself a design point worth raising. Non-functional requirements are the exam: EXPECTED O(1) for get, put and remove — say expected, and say that it assumes a hash that distributes keys roughly uniformly and a bounded load factor; MEMORY proportional to the number of entries with a tunable space/time dial; PREDICTABLE GROWTH, since the table must expand as entries are added without degrading into long chains, and the cost of that expansion must be characterised honestly as amortised; and DEFINED ITERATION SEMANTICS — no ordering guarantee at all (so callers cannot depend on it), and fail-fast behaviour on structural modification during iteration so a bug surfaces as an exception instead of as a silently skipped entry. Explicitly out of scope in the first pass but worth flagging as the natural follow-up: thread safety, which changes the design substantially (per-bucket locking or CAS, and a resize protocol that concurrent readers can survive). Also clarify expected size up front, because pre-sizing the table avoids the resize entirely and is the single cheapest optimisation available."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "TABLE is a Node array whose length is a power of two — not for elegance but because it makes the bucket index a bitmask, index = (n - 1) & hash, instead of a modulo division. NODE is the entry: the key, the value, the CACHED HASH (storing it avoids recomputing a possibly expensive hashCode on every comparison and lets a mismatch be rejected by an int compare before ever calling equals — a small field with a large effect), and, under separate chaining, a next pointer forming the bucket's linked list. TREENODE is Java 8's escalation: when a bucket's chain exceeds a threshold (8, with the table at least 64 long) the chain is converted into a small RED-BLACK TREE ordered by hash then by key, converting that bucket's worst case from O(n) to O(log n); it un-treeifies below 6 to avoid thrashing at the boundary. SIZE is the live entry count and THRESHOLD is capacity * loadFactor, the precomputed trigger so put does one int comparison rather than a multiplication. HASH SPREADING is a small function, not a class, but it is a real responsibility: h ^ (h >>> 16) mixes the high bits of the key's hashCode down into the low bits, because masking with (n - 1) looks ONLY at the low bits and a hashCode whose entropy lives high would otherwise collide catastrophically. MODCOUNT is the structural-modification counter that makes iterators fail fast. The map object owns the table, size, threshold, load factor and modCount, and exposes no internal node type. Under OPEN ADDRESSING the entity list changes shape: there are no Node chains, entries live directly in the array, and a TOMBSTONE marker becomes a first-class entity because deletion cannot simply blank a slot."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "Three decisions define the design and each has a defensible alternative, which is exactly what makes this a design interview rather than a coding exercise. DECISION ONE, COLLISION RESOLUTION. SEPARATE CHAINING puts colliding entries in a per-bucket list (or tree): simple, tolerant of load factors above 1, deletion is a trivial unlink, and performance degrades gracefully. Its costs are a node object per entry (allocation, header overhead, pointer chasing) and poor cache locality, since each chain step is likely a cache miss. OPEN ADDRESSING with LINEAR PROBING stores entries directly in the array and, on collision, walks forward to the next free slot: no per-entry allocation, and outstanding CACHE LOCALITY because the probe sequence is sequential memory — often twice as fast in practice at low load factors, which is why performance-focused libraries and languages (Python's dict, Rust's hashbrown, most game engines) choose it. Its costs are real: it requires the load factor to stay well below 1 (typically 0.5 to 0.7) or clustering makes probe sequences long; it is more sensitive to a poor hash, since clusters merge; and DELETION IS GENUINELY HARD, which is the tombstone problem in the deep dive. DECISION TWO, THE LOAD FACTOR AND RESIZE POLICY: 0.75 is the standard compromise between wasted array slots and chain length, threshold = capacity * loadFactor, and growth is DOUBLING so the power-of-two masking survives. DECISION THREE, WHAT TO DO ABOUT A BAD HASH: spread the bits, and treeify pathological buckets. Structurally, keep the class monolithic — a HashMap with a private Node type and private helpers for hash, index, resize and treeify. Resist a CollisionStrategy interface: the two strategies have incompatible internal layouts (chains versus probe sequences versus tombstones), so making them pluggable means an abstraction that leaks in every method, and naming that trap is better design commentary than building it. What SHOULD be injectable is the initial capacity and load factor, because those are the genuine tuning surface, and pre-sizing to expectedSize / 0.75 + 1 is the standard advice worth stating."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java) — separate chaining with power-of-two masking",
        "class HashMap<K, V> {\n  static class Node<K, V> {\n    final int hash; final K key; V value; Node<K, V> next;   // hash CACHED\n    Node(int h, K k, V v, Node<K, V> n) { hash = h; key = k; value = v; next = n; }\n  }\n\n  private Node<K, V>[] table;\n  private int size, threshold, modCount;\n  private final float loadFactor;\n\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  HashMap(int initialCapacity, float loadFactor) {\n    int cap = Integer.highestOneBit(Math.max(1, initialCapacity - 1)) << 1;  // 2^k\n    this.table = new Node[cap];\n    this.loadFactor = loadFactor;\n    this.threshold = (int) (cap * loadFactor);\n  }\n\n  /** Mix high bits down: the mask only reads the LOW bits of the hash. */\n  private static int spread(Object key) {\n    if (key == null) return 0;\n    int h = key.hashCode();\n    return h ^ (h >>> 16);\n  }\n\n  private int indexFor(int hash) { return (table.length - 1) & hash; }   // not %\n\n  public V get(Object key) {\n    int h = spread(key);\n    for (Node<K, V> n = table[indexFor(h)]; n != null; n = n.next)\n      if (n.hash == h && Objects.equals(n.key, key))   // cheap int test FIRST\n        return n.value;\n    return null;                                       // expected O(1); O(n) if\n  }                                                    // the bucket degenerates\n\n  public V put(K key, V value) {\n    int h = spread(key);\n    int i = indexFor(h);\n    for (Node<K, V> n = table[i]; n != null; n = n.next)\n      if (n.hash == h && Objects.equals(n.key, key)) { V old = n.value; n.value = value; return old; }\n    table[i] = new Node<>(h, key, value, table[i]);     // prepend, O(1)\n    modCount++;\n    if (++size > threshold) resize();                   // amortised O(1) per put\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  public V remove(Object key) {\n    int h = spread(key);\n    int i = indexFor(h);\n    Node<K, V> prev = null;\n    for (Node<K, V> n = table[i]; n != null; prev = n, n = n.next) {\n      if (n.hash == h && Objects.equals(n.key, key)) {\n        if (prev == null) table[i] = n.next; else prev.next = n.next;   // unlink:\n        size--; modCount++;                          // no tombstone needed under\n        return n.value;                              // separate chaining\n      }\n    }\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  /** Doubling rehash: O(n) once per O(n) inserts, hence amortised O(1). */\n  @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n  private void resize() {\n    Node<K, V>[] old = table;\n    Node<K, V>[] neu = new Node[old.length << 1];\n    for (Node<K, V> head : old) {\n      for (Node<K, V> n = head; n != null; ) {\n        Node<K, V> next = n.next;\n        int i = (neu.length - 1) & n.hash;   // reuses the CACHED hash: no rehash\n        n.next = neu[i]; neu[i] = n;         // of the key object itself\n        n = next;\n      }\n    }\n    table = neu;\n    threshold = (int) (neu.length * loadFactor);\n  }\n\n  public Iterator<K> keyIterator() {\n    final int expected = modCount;\n    return new Iterator<>() {\n      // ... advance across buckets and chains ...\n      public boolean hasNext() { check(); return false; }\n      public K next() { check(); throw new NoSuchElementException(); }\n      private void check() {\n        if (modCount != expected) throw new ConcurrentModificationException();  // fail fast\n      }\n    };\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: chaining vs open addressing, and the tombstone problem deletion creates",
        "The collision decision is the heart of the design, and the deletion asymmetry is the part candidates rarely reach. SEPARATE CHAINING: each bucket holds a list, put prepends in O(1), get walks the chain comparing cached hashes first, remove unlinks a node. Deletion is TRIVIAL — the chain is a linked list and removing from it leaves nothing behind. Load factors above 1 are survivable (the chains just get longer), memory is one node object per entry plus the array, and the failure mode is graceful: a bad hash makes one chain long, not the whole table useless. OPEN ADDRESSING: entries live in the array itself, and a collision sends the probe forward — linear probing (i+1, i+2, ...) for locality, quadratic probing or double hashing to break up clusters. Lookups walk the probe sequence until they find the key or hit an EMPTY slot, and that stopping rule is the crux. Now the deletion problem. Suppose keys A and B both hash to slot 5; A takes slot 5, B probes forward to slot 6. Delete A and blank slot 5. Now look up B: the probe starts at 5, finds an EMPTY slot, and concludes B is not present — a slot that was merely vacated has BROKEN THE PROBE CHAIN, and B is now unreachable even though it sits one slot away. The standard fix is the TOMBSTONE: mark the vacated slot DELETED rather than EMPTY, so lookups continue probing through it while insertions may reuse it. That works, and it introduces its own decay — tombstones count as occupied for the purpose of probe length but hold no data, so a workload with many deletes accumulates them until probe sequences are long and lookups slow down even though the map is nearly empty. The remedies are to count tombstones and trigger a REHASH when they exceed a fraction of the table (cleaning them all out), or to use BACKWARD-SHIFT DELETION, which walks forward from the hole and moves back any entry whose ideal position is at or before the hole, restoring the probe invariant with no tombstones at all — this is what Robin Hood hashing implementations do, and mentioning it is a strong signal. The design conclusion to state: chaining is the safer default and what the JDK ships; open addressing is measurably faster at low load factors and in cache-sensitive code, but you are signing up for tombstone management or backward-shift deletion, plus keeping the load factor well under 1 because clustering grows probe lengths superlinearly as the table fills."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: load factor, amortised resize, and why incremental rehashing exists",
        "The load factor alpha = size / capacity is the dial that keeps the expected O(1) honest. Under chaining with a uniform hash, the expected chain length is exactly alpha, so a lookup costs 1 + alpha/2 comparisons on average — constant as long as alpha is bounded. Let alpha grow without bound and every bucket becomes a list; the map degrades into a set of linked lists and the O(1) claim evaporates. Hence the THRESHOLD: when size exceeds capacity * loadFactor (0.75 in the JDK, chosen because it balances wasted slots against collision probability, with the birthday-problem intuition that collisions become common well before the table is full), the table DOUBLES and every entry is redistributed. THE AMORTISED ARGUMENT, which the interviewer wants stated properly: a resize is O(n), but it only happens after roughly n/2 insertions since the previous one, and each doubling is twice as expensive as the last while also buying twice as much runway. Summing the resize costs across n insertions gives n + n/2 + n/4 + ... < 2n, so the TOTAL cost of n puts is O(n) and the AMORTISED cost per put is O(1). Say amortised, and then say what it does not mean: it is not a per-operation guarantee. The unlucky put that triggers the rehash of a two-million-entry map does real O(n) work — allocating a new array, walking every chain, and (if the map is large) provoking a GC pause on top. In a latency-sensitive service that single put is a multi-millisecond tail-latency spike that shows up as a p99.9 cliff and is genuinely hard to diagnose. That is why INCREMENTAL REHASHING exists. Redis does it explicitly: on resize it allocates the new table but keeps the old one, and every subsequent operation migrates a few buckets from old to new while lookups check BOTH tables until the migration completes. The total work is the same but it is spread over many operations, converting one huge pause into a small constant tax on each — a classic latency-versus-throughput trade, and exactly the kind of engineering judgment senior interviews are looking for. Two practical corollaries worth stating. First, PRE-SIZE the map when you know the count: new HashMap<>(expected / 0.75 + 1) skips every intermediate resize, which is the cheapest performance win in the entire structure and something most code gets wrong. Second, the cached hash in each Node means a resize never calls hashCode again — it re-masks the stored int — so resizing an expensive-to-hash key type (long strings) is far cheaper than it looks, and with a power-of-two table each entry either stays at index i or moves to i + oldCapacity, which is the split-in-two optimisation Java 8 uses to avoid recomputing indices entirely."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the equals/hashCode contract, hash flooding, and iteration semantics",
        "THE CONTRACT IS THE FOUNDATION, and it is a one-way implication that must be stated exactly: if two objects are equal, they MUST have the same hashCode; if two objects have the same hashCode they need not be equal. Violate the first direction and the map is simply broken — put(k, v) stores the entry in bucket A, and a later get with an equal key computes a different hash, looks in bucket B, and returns null for a key that is demonstrably present. The classic production version of this bug is a MUTABLE KEY: insert an object into the map, then mutate a field that participates in hashCode, and the entry becomes unreachable — it is in the map, iteration will show it, and get will never find it, because it now hashes to a different bucket than the one it lives in. The design rule to state is that map keys should be immutable, or at least immutable in their hash-relevant fields. The second direction, many keys sharing a hash, is legal and merely costs performance — until it is weaponised. HASH FLOODING is a real denial-of-service attack: an attacker who knows your hash function crafts thousands of distinct keys that collide into one bucket (trivially done for Java's String hash), submits them as JSON fields or form parameters, and every insert walks the growing chain, so parsing an n-key request costs O(n^2) and a few megabytes of payload burns a CPU core for minutes. This took down web frameworks across every major language in 2011 and 2012. The mitigations are three, and knowing which applies where is the point: SEEDED or RANDOMISED hashing per process (SipHash in Python, Rust and Perl) so an attacker cannot precompute collisions — the strongest fix, but it changes iteration order between runs, which is why Java did not take it for HashMap's contract; TREEIFICATION, Java 8's answer, converting a bucket to a red-black tree once its chain exceeds 8 nodes in a table of at least 64, capping the worst case at O(log n) — it does not prevent the collisions, it bounds their cost; and simply validating or bounding untrusted input sizes. Finally ITERATION SEMANTICS, which is a design decision, not an accident: a hash map guarantees NO ORDER, and any code depending on the order observed in testing will break on resize (which redistributes everything) or on a JDK upgrade. Java's HashMap gives a fail-fast iterator: a modCount incremented on every structural change lets the iterator throw ConcurrentModificationException rather than silently skipping or duplicating entries — a best-effort bug-detection mechanism, explicitly not a synchronisation guarantee. If order matters, the right answer is a different type: LinkedHashMap for insertion or access order (which is also the one-line LRU cache), TreeMap for sorted order."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "CHAINING vs OPEN ADDRESSING: chaining tolerates load factors above 1, deletes cleanly, degrades gracefully under a bad hash, and costs a node allocation plus pointer chasing per entry; open addressing has excellent cache locality and no per-entry object, but needs alpha well under 1, is far more sensitive to clustering, and forces you to solve deletion with tombstones (which accumulate and must be cleaned by rehash) or with backward-shift deletion. LOAD FACTOR as a dial: lower means more memory and fewer collisions, higher means the opposite; 0.75 is the JDK's balance, 0.5 is typical for open addressing, and the number should be a construction parameter rather than a constant baked into the class. RESIZE POLICY: stop-the-world doubling gives amortised O(1) with a tail-latency spike proportional to the map's size; incremental rehashing (Redis-style dual tables) does the same total work spread across operations, trading a little complexity and a slower steady state for bounded pause times — choose by whether p99.9 latency or peak throughput is the requirement. HASH MAP vs BALANCED TREE: expected O(1) versus worst-case O(log n), and no ordering versus sorted iteration, range and neighbour queries — the tree is not a slower map, it is a map whose API includes order. HASH MAP vs ARRAY: when keys are dense small integers, an array beats every hash map on both memory and speed, and noticing that is a good instinct to display. CONCURRENCY, the standard follow-up: a synchronized wrapper serialises everything; ConcurrentHashMap instead uses per-bin CAS for the common case and synchronises on the bin head only when a collision must be resolved, and its resize is COOPERATIVE — threads that arrive during a resize help transfer bins rather than block — which is a beautiful illustration of the incremental-rehash idea under concurrency. EXTENSIONS worth naming: LinkedHashMap threading a doubly-linked list through the entries for insertion or access order and, with removeEldestEntry, an LRU cache in a few lines; weak or soft reference keys for caches (with the caveat that identity versus equality semantics change); open-addressed primitive-specialised maps to escape boxing entirely when keys are ints; and CONSISTENT HASHING, which is the same collision-and-distribution reasoning lifted from buckets in an array to shards in a cluster — the natural bridge from this LLD question into a system-design one."),
    ],
    "Design-a-HashMap is the most common data-structure interview in existence, and the discriminator is precision about the bound. The expected answer is not 'O(1)' but 'EXPECTED O(1), assuming a hash that distributes well and a bounded load factor, with an O(n) worst case when a bucket degenerates — which Java 8 caps at O(log n) by treeifying chains past 8'. From there interviewers probe three places: the collision decision (separate chaining versus open addressing, with cache locality and load-factor consequences, and the tombstone problem that deletion creates under probing); the resize story (why doubling is amortised O(1) via the geometric sum, and why latency-sensitive systems rehash incrementally instead of stopping the world); and the equals/hashCode contract, including mutable keys becoming unreachable and hash flooding as a genuine DoS. Iteration semantics — no ordering guarantee, fail-fast via modCount — is the fourth, and it is where candidates who have only used a hash map get separated from those who have designed one.",
    [
      G("unqualified_o1", "Claiming O(1) for get and put without qualification, and having no answer for what happens when many keys collide.", "State it as EXPECTED O(1), conditional on a well-distributed hash and a bounded load factor: the expected chain length is exactly the load factor, so the constant holds only while alpha is bounded, and a degenerate hash collapses a bucket to O(n) — which Java 8 bounds at O(log n) by treeifying chains longer than 8 in tables of at least 64.", "complexity stated as O(1) with no worst case; no mention of load factor; no answer to 'what if hashCode returns a constant' or to hash-flooding DoS."),
      G("open_addressing_delete", "Implementing open addressing and deleting an entry by blanking its slot, silently breaking the probe chain for every key that probed past it.", "A vacated slot must become a TOMBSTONE (probes continue through it, insertions may reuse it) with a rehash when tombstones accumulate, or use backward-shift deletion to move later entries into the hole and restore the probe invariant with no tombstones at all.", "remove() setting the array slot to null under linear probing; keys becoming unfindable after unrelated deletions; no discussion of tombstones or of why deletion is harder under probing than under chaining."),
      G("resize_amortization", "Describing resize as if it were free, or conversely calling put O(n) because it sometimes rehashes — in both cases missing the amortised argument and its latency caveat.", "Give the geometric sum: a doubling rehash is O(n) but occurs only after O(n) insertions, so total cost over n puts is under 2n and the amortised cost per put is O(1) — then add the caveat that amortised is not per-operation, so a large stop-the-world rehash is a real tail-latency spike, which is why Redis-style incremental rehashing (dual tables, a few buckets migrated per operation) exists.", "no mention of load factor threshold or doubling; claiming put is worst-case O(1); no awareness that a huge rehash causes a p99 spike; not pre-sizing a map whose size is known in advance."),
    ],
    0.5, DIAG.hashmap, "Hash map class diagram"),
];

const xpFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 5 : l === "easy" ? 10 : l === "medium" ? 15 : 20);
const diffFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 0.1 : l === "easy" ? 0.2 : l === "medium" ? 0.4 : 0.6);
function pm(moduleId, o) {
  return { trackKey: TRACK_KEY, moduleId, topicId: o.topicId, exerciseId: o.exerciseId,
    position: o.position, level: o.level, type: "pattern_match", title: o.title, scenario: o.scenario,
    instructions: "Pick the best answer.", starterCode: "", expectedSolution: o.correct,
    blanks: [{ options: o.options }], testCases: [{ type: "pattern_match", correct: o.correct, explanation: o.explanation }],
    hints: [], xpReward: xpFor(o.level), difficulty: diffFor(o.level) };
}

